* #2250 #2281 #2572 "清解餘毒；清透伏邪" "A treatment method to clear heat and remove hidden toxins."
* #2250 #2281 #2573 "斂瘡止痛" "A treatment method to alleviate pain and speed up ulcerative wound healing."
* #2250 #2281 #2574 "以毒攻毒" "A treatment method to remove toxins with poisonous medicines."
* #2250 #2281 #2575 "提膿拔毒" "A treatment method to drain pus through therapies or formulas to draw out pus and toxin."
* #2250 #2281 #2576 "提膿祛腐" "A treatment method to drain pus and eliminate dead tissues through therapies or formulas to draw pus and remove putridity."
* #2250 #2281 #2577 "治五官法" "Treatment methods for problems of the eyes, noses lips, tongue and ears"
* #2250 #2281 #2578 "明目" "A treatment method to remove wind, clear heat, cool blood, transform stasis, nourish blood, supplement qi, nourish yin, strengthen the spleen and tonify the liver and kidney to remove pathogenic factors, reinforce anti-pathogenic qi and benefit the eyes. It is indicated for blurred vision."
* #2250 #2281 #2579 "通耳；利耳" "A treatment method to remove pathogenic factors, reinforce anti-pathogenic qi and benefit the ears."
* #2250 #2281 #2580 "通鼻" "A treatment method to remove pathogenic factors, reinforce anti-pathogenic qi and benefit the nose."
* #2250 #2281 #2581 "利咽" "A treatment method to remove pathogenic factors, reinforce anti-pathogenic qi and benefit the throat."
* #2250 #2281 #2582 "開音" "A treatment method to remove pathogenic factors, reinforce anti-pathogenic qi and restore the voice. It is indicated for hoarseness."
* #2250 #2281 #2583 "固齒" "A treatment method to remove pathogenic factors, reinforce anti-pathogenic qi, secure the teeth and moisten the gums."
* #2250 #2281 #2584 "其他治法"
* #2250 #2281 #2585 "壯水制陽；滋陰抑陽" "A treatment method to nourish yin fluids to contain hyperactivity of yang qi. It is indicated for hyperactivity of yang due to yin deficiency."
* #2250 #2281 #2586 "益火消陰；壯陽消陰" "A treatment method to warm and reinforce yang qi to dissipate yin cold. It is indicated for yin cold due to yang deficiency."
* #2250 #2281 #2587 "引火歸原；導龍入海" "A treatment method to reinforce kidney yang and combine medicines that enter meridians to astringe floating yang qi, i.e. to guide kidney yang into kidney yin. It is indicated for decline of vital fire or floating of deficiency yang."
* #2250 #2281 #2588 "和營生新" "A treatment method to harmonize between the Ying nutrients and blood, circulate blood, regenerate new blood and flesh, and speed up wound healing. It is indicated for delayed wound healing due to blood stagnation."
* #2250 #2281 #2589 "宣通三焦" "A treatment method to regulate qi, eliminate pathogenic factors and unblock the three jiao. It is indicated for pathogenic factors impairing the qi flow of the three jiao."
* #2250 #2281 #2590 "交通心腎" "A treatment method to nourish kidney yin, astringe kidney yang, reduce heart fire and calm the heart mind to submerge yang and coordinate between the heart and kidney. It is indicated for conditions due to disharmony between the heart fire and kidney water."
* #2250 #2281 #2591 "清利頭目" "A treatment method to clear heat, reduce fire, submerge yang and clear the head and eyes. It is indicated for headache, eye pain and dizziness due to upward flaming of fire heat or hyperactivity of yang qi."
* #2250 #2281 #2592 "滑利竅道" "A treatment method to lubricate and unblock orifices. It is indicated for dry orifices due to insufficiency of yin fluids."
* #2250 #2281 #2593 "寬胸利膈" "A treatment method to eliminate pathogenic factors, regulate qi, lift yang, and down-regulate turbidity to soothe the chest and diaphragm. It is indicated for chest stuffiness, distension, fullness and tightness."
* #2250 #2281 #2594 "續筋接骨" "A treatment method to use tuina or other therapies to repair the broken sinews or bones. It is indicated for sinew or bone injuries."
* #2250 #2281 #2595 "強筋壯骨" "A treatment method to strengthen the sinews or bones through exercise or taking medicine. It is indicated for weak sinews/bones due to heredity, trauma or chronic diseases."
* #2250 #2281 #2596 "散結；軟堅" "A treatment method to eliminate phlegm, regulate qi, break stasis and unblock collaterals to soften hardness, disperses nodules and resolve masses. It is indicated for masses, lumps or swelling."
* #2250 #2281 #2597 "佐金平木" "A treatment method to disperse the lung (metal) and inhibit the liver (wood). It is indicated for migratory pain in the subcostal region, cough or panting due to hyperactivity of liver yang and failure of lung qi to disperse and descend."
* #2250 #2281 #2598 "益火生土" "A treatment method to warm and reinforce the vital fire to supplement the transportation and transformation of the spleen (earth). It is indicated for early morning diarrhoea due to decline of the vital fire and spleen yang deficiency."
* #2250 #2281 #2599 "金水相生" "A treatment method to tonify the kidney and nourish the lung for lung deficiency and kidney deficiency, since the lung (metal) and kidney (water) mutually support physiologically and mutually affect pathologically."
* #2250 #2281 #2600 "培土制水" "A treatment method to tonify the spleen (earth) to restrain overflow of water dampness. It is indicated for oedema or diarrhoea due to spleen deficiency."
* #2250 #2281 #2601 "瀉南補北" "A treatment method to reduce heart fire and reinforce kidney water. The south here refers to heart fire, whereas the north refers to kidney water."
* #2250 #2602 "3.3 藥物治療類"
* #2250 #2602 #2603 "中藥類" "Knowledge about the concept of Chinese medicines, the basic theories as well as the origin, production, processing, property, actions and indications of medicines"
* #2250 #2602 #2604 "中藥" "Medicines to prevent, treat and diagnose diseases under the guidance of Chinese medical theories."
* #2250 #2602 #2605 "中藥學" "The study of basic theories, origin, production, processing, property, actions, indications and application of Chinese medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2606 "本草" "An ancient term for Chinese medicine, mainly referring to medicinal plants."
* #2250 #2602 #2607 "草藥" "Regional herbal medicines that may not be recorded in major books on materia medica; however, they are an integral part of Chinese medicine."
* #2250 #2602 #2608 "中藥材" "Raw materials include medicinal plants, animals and minerals that can be prepared into pieces, extracts or Chinese patent medicine."
* #2250 #2602 #2609 "道地藥材" "High-quality Chinese medicines produced in specific geographical areas."
* #2250 #2602 #2610 "飲片" "Prepared herbal pieces that can be used directly in clinical use or preparation production."
* #2250 #2602 #2611 "配方顆粒" "A new dosage form with standardized specification, dosage and quality manufactured through extracting, separating, condensing, drying, granulating or packaging."
* #2250 #2602 #2612 "炮製" "A sequence of medicinal processing techniques to make materia medica into prepared pieces or different dosage forms. These techniques are adopted according to theories of Chinese medicine and medicinal properties."
* #2250 #2602 #2613 "修治；修制" "The preliminary processing method of Chinese medicines, including purification, crushing and cutting."
* #2250 #2602 #2614 "純淨處理" "A method to remove dust, impurities and clean medicines by picking, selecting, sieving, scraping and brushing."
* #2250 #2602 #2615 "粉碎處理" "A method to grind medicines by pounding, grinding, filing and crushing."
* #2250 #2602 #2616 "切制處理" "A method to cut medicines into pieces, blocks, threads or segments for preparation, drying, storage and weighing."
* #2250 #2602 #2617 "水制" "A processing method to use water or other liquid auxiliary materials, including washing, bleaching, soaking, refining with water, etc."
* #2250 #2602 #2618 "潤；悶；伏" "A method to moisten and soften medicines with water or other liquids before cutting, including water spraying, washing, soaking, infiltrating, drying, etc."
* #2250 #2602 #2619 "水飛" "A method to obtain extremely fine medicinal powder in water."
* #2250 #2602 #2620 "漂" "A method to soak medicines in static or running water for a period of time and exchange water regularly to remove the fishy smell, salt or toxic ingredients."
* #2250 #2602 #2621 "火制" "A processing method to use heat or fire, including frying, roasting, calcining, etc."
* #2250 #2602 #2622 "炒" "A method to stir fry medicines in a pot to a certain temperature degree, including plain frying or frying with adjuvants."
* #2250 #2602 #2623 "清炒" "A method to fry without adjuvants. According to the temperature, plain stir-frying is divided into fried yellow, scorch fry and charcoaled fry."
* #2250 #2602 #2624 "輔料" "These include liquid materials such as alcohol, vinegar or honey and non-liquid materials such as starch, sugar or salt. The stir-fry method involves medicines and solid materials. These include stir- frying with bran, rice, soil, sand, talcum powder or clamshell powder."
* #2250 #2602 #2625 "炙" "A method to use liquid adjuvants to stir-fry medicines to change the medicinal property, reinforce the efficacy and reduce side-effects. The excipients include wine, vinegar, salt, ginger, honey or oil."
* #2250 #2602 #2626 "煆" "A method to calcine medicines directly or indirectly with a strong fire to fully extract the active ingredients and achieve maximal efficacy."
* #2250 #2602 #2627 "煨" "A method to wrap medicines with water-mixed flour or wet paper, placed in the heat of the ash, or heated with oil-absorbent paper separated from the drug barrier so that the medicine is turned from raw to cooked."
* #2250 #2602 #2628 "水火共制" "A processing method to wrap medicines with wet tissue and place into fire or isolate with oil- absorbing sheets."
* #2250 #2602 #2629 "煮" "A method to heat medicines with water or liquid excipients."
* #2250 #2602 #2630 "蒸" "A method to steam or heat with water."
* #2250 #2602 #2631 "燀" "A method to blanch in boiling water and take out immediately for drying process."
* #2250 #2602 #2632 "淬" "A method to calcine medicines and place into cold water or liquid excipient quickly for easy crushing and absorption."
* #2250 #2602 #2633 "中藥變質" "Physical or chemical changes in Chinese medicines due to inappropriate care in transportation or storage. These changes include mildew, worm-eaten, discolouration, flavour reversion, oiliness or air-slake and can directly affect the medicinal quality, efficacy and safety."
* #2250 #2602 #2634 "中藥不良反應" "These include side-effects or toxic reactions. Following the standard dosage, side-effects of Chinese medicines are often mild and can resolve spontaneously without treatment. Toxic reactions are often caused by overdose or prolonged use of poisonous materia medica, and can do substantial damage to the organs and functions."
* #2250 #2602 #2635 "藥物偏性" "Peculiar features and actions of medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2636 "中藥性能" "The core part of essential theories on Chinese medicines. These include four properties, five flavours, ascending/floating, descending/sinking, meridian tropism and toxicity."
* #2250 #2602 #2637 "中藥性狀" "Physical or chemical characteristics of Chinese medicines, including their shape, colour, odour, taste and properties."
* #2250 #2602 #2638 "四氣五味" "The basic properties and flavours of Chinese medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2639 "四氣；四性" "The four basic properties of medicines—cold, hot, warmth, and coolness."
* #2250 #2602 #2640 "溫熱藥" "Medicines to remove or alleviate cold-induced disorders."
* #2250 #2602 #2641 "寒涼藥" "Medicines to remove or alleviate heat-induced disorders."
* #2250 #2602 #2642 "平性藥" "Medicines with no obvious property of cold or heat."
* #2250 #2602 #2643 "辛味" "Medicines with a pungent flavour act to disperse/ circulate qi and move blood. They are indicated for exterior pattern, qi stagnation and blood stasis."
* #2250 #2602 #2644 "苦味" "Medicines with a bitter flavour act to unblock, purge the intestine, dry dampness, clear fire and benefit yin. They are indicated for fire heat, dampness, adverse flow of qi and fire hyperactivity due to yin deficiency."
* #2250 #2602 #2645 "甘味" "Medicines with a sweet flavour act to tonify, harmonize the middle jiao and relieve spasm. They are indicated for deficiency pattern, disharmony between the spleen and stomach, spasm, pain, etc."
* #2250 #2602 #2646 "酸味" "Medicines with a sour flavour act to astringe and secure. They are indicated for sweating (due to deficiency), diarrhoea, frequent urination, enuresis, bleeding, etc. Some sour-flavoured medicines can generate fluids, calm worms and stop pain."
* #2250 #2602 #2647 "澀味" "Medicines with an astringent flavour act to astringe and secure. They are indicated for sweating (due to deficiency), diarrhoea, frequent urination, enuresis, bleeding, etc."
* #2250 #2602 #2648 "鹹味" "Medicines with a salty flavour act to purge the intestine, promote bowel movements and soften masses. They are indicated for constipation, goitre, scrofula, phlegm nodules and masses."
* #2250 #2602 #2649 "淡味" "Medicines with a bland flavour act to drain dampness and promote urination. They are indicated for oedema, dysuria and leg swelling."
* #2250 #2602 #2650 "辛甘化陽" "The combination of a pungent and sweet flavour engenders a dispersing, outward and yang- assisting function. This includes a single medicine with both flavours or combined pungent and sweet medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2651 "酸甘化陰" "The combination of a sour and sweet flavour engenders a fluid-generating, inward and astringing function. This includes a single medicinal with both flavours or combined sour and sweet medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2652 "辛開苦降" "The combination of a pungent and bitter flavour allows for dispersal of obstruction and the descent of qi. This includes a single medicine with both flavours or combined pungent and bitter medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2653 "升降浮沉" "The four cardinal directionalities of medicines: ascending, descending, sinking and floating. These four directions are associated with the weight, property, preparation and combination of medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2654 "升浮藥" "Medicines to lift yang, release the exterior, induce vomiting, open the orifices, etc. They tend to go upward and outward."
* #2250 #2602 #2655 "沉降藥" "Medicines to purge the intestine, resolve water retention, submerge yang and astringe. They tend to go inward and downward."
* #2250 #2602 #2656 "歸經" "The ability of medicines to access a channel/ meridian. Meridian affinity is based on the theories on zang–fu organs and meridians."
* #2250 #2602 #2657 "引經報使" "A property that medicines have special role for certain zang–fu organs or meridians and can guide other medicines to reach the affected meridians and body parts."
* #2250 #2602 #2658 "引經藥" "Medicines with the ability to guide other ingredients of a formula to access a particular channel/meridian."
* #2250 #2602 #2659 "配伍" "The combination, according to Chinese medicine theory, of two or more medicines to achieve a pre- determined clinical outcome."
* #2250 #2602 #2660 "七情和合" "Compatibility methods of Chinese medicines, including the using of a single medicine, mutual reinforcement, mutual assistance, mutual restraint, mutual suppression, mutual inhibition and mutual opposition."
* #2250 #2602 #2661 "單行" "Use a single medicine alone to treat a disorder."
* #2250 #2602 #2662 "相須" "Use two medicines sharing similar properties in combination to reinforce each other’s action."
* #2250 #2602 #2663 "相使" "Use two or more medicines sharing common efficacies in combination, one being the principal medicine while the others play a subsidiary role to reinforce the action of the principal one."
* #2250 #2602 #2664 "相畏" "When two medicines are used in combination, the toxic or side-effects of one medicine can be reduced or eliminated by the other."
* #2250 #2602 #2665 "相殺" "When two medicines are used in combination, one medicine can reduce or eliminate the toxic or side- effects of the other."
* #2250 #2602 #2666 "相惡" "When two medicines are used in combination, the positive effects of one medicine can be reduced or eliminated by the other."
* #2250 #2602 #2667 "相反" "When two medicines are used in combination, their toxic or side-effects can be produced or enhanced."
* #2250 #2602 #2668 "配伍禁忌" "Combination of some medicines can reduce the curative effect, produce or enhance toxic or side- effects and/or affect the safety of medicines. These combinations should be avoided. Specifically, there are eighteen antagonisms and nineteen incompatibilities."
* #2250 #2602 #2669 "十八反" "One of the contraindicated combinations of Chinese medicines. These include: Gan Cao (Radix Glycyrrhizae) is antagonistic to Da Ji (Radix Euphorbiae Pekinensis), Yuan Hua (Flos Genkwa), Gan Sui (Radix Euphorbiae Kansui) and Hai Zao (Sargassum); Wu Tou (Radix Aconiti) is antagonistic to Bei Mu (Bulbus Fritillariae), Gua Lou (Fructus Trichosanthis), Ban Xia (Rhizoma Pinelliae), Bai Lian (Radix Ampelopsis) and Bai Ji (Rhizoma Bletillae); and Li Lu (Radix Veratri Nigri) is antagonistic to Ren Shen (Radix Ginseng), Sha Shen (Radix Salviae Miltiorrhizae), Dan Shen (Radix Adenophorae), Ku Shen (Radix Sophorae Flavescentis), Xuan Shen (Radix Scrophulariae), Xi Xin (Herba Asari) and Shao Yao (Radix Paeoniae)."
* #2250 #2602 #2670 "十九畏" "One of the contraindicated combinations of Chinese medicines. These include: Liu Huang (sulfur) is incompatible with Pu Xiao (crude sodium sulfate), Shui Yin (mercury) with Pi Shuang (arsenic trioxide),Lang Du (Radix Euphorbiae Ebracteolatae) Mi Tuo Seng (litharge), Ba Dou (Semen Crotonis) with Qian Niu (Semen Pharbitidis), Ding Xiang (Flos Caryophylli) with Yu Jin (Radix Curcumae), Ya Xiao (crystalline sodium sulfate) with San Leng (Rhizoma Sparganii), Chuan Wu (Radix Aconiti) and Cao Wu (Radix Aconiti Kuznezoffi) with Xi Jiao (Cornu Rhinoceri), Ren Shen (Radix Ginseng) with Wu Ling Zhi (with Faeces Trogopterorum), and Guan Gui (Cortex Cinnamomi) with Shi Zhi (Halloysitum Rubrum)."
* #2250 #2602 #2671 "證候用藥禁忌；病證用藥禁忌" "Medicines that may aggravate medical conditions should be avoided."
* #2250 #2602 #2672 "妊娠用藥禁忌" "Medicines that are absolutely contraindicated to use or used with caution during pregnancy. Absolutely contraindicated medicines are toxic, harsh or abortive. Medicines to circulate blood, move qi, and drain downward, and warm, pungent medicines to warm the interior should be used with caution."
* #2250 #2602 #2673 "服藥食忌" "Food that should be avoided while taking medication."
* #2250 #2602 #2674 "湯液" "A liquid made by decocting prepared pieces of Chinese medicines in water."
* #2250 #2602 #2675 "煎藥法" "The decoction of Chinese medicine involves the pot, water, heat control, and decocting method."
* #2250 #2602 #2676 "煎藥火候" "This includes the heat intensity and decocting duration. It includes quick (strong) fire and slow (mild) fire. Generally, a quick fire is used to boil and then reduced to mild fire (a simmering heat) to maintain the temperature."
* #2250 #2602 #2677 "文火；慢火；微火" "A mild and slow fire to maintain the temperature in decocting Chinese medicine after boiling."
* #2250 #2602 #2678 "武火；急火；緊火" "A strong and quick fire in decocting Chinese medicine."
* #2250 #2602 #2679 "先煎" "A method for insoluble minerals, rocks or shells that require a long time to decoct. These ingredients need to be ground and decocted 30–60 minutes first before placing into the rest of the decoction. Toxic medicines need to decoct first for 60–120 minutes before placing into the rest of the decoction to reduce their toxicity."
* #2250 #2602 #2680 "另煎；另燉" "A method to decoct some medicines separately (2–3 hours), usually precious medicines, to ensure that the ingredients remain active."
* #2250 #2602 #2681 "沖服" "A method to pour the medicine directly into water or other boiling liquid. Valuable medicines need to be ground into fine powder first."
* #2250 #2602 #2682 "泡服；焗服" "A method to soak medicines in hot water or decoctions for half an hour and then take the medication after removing the dregs."
* #2250 #2602 #2683 "煎湯代水" "A method to decoct some ingredients separately and then use the decoction instead of water to decoct the rest of the ingredients. This method has two purposes: 1) to prevent the separately decocted ingredients causing the decoction to appear cloudy or muddy; 2) applies to ingredients that are light in weight, big in dosage and volume, and strong in water absorption."
* #2250 #2602 #2684 "解表藥" "Medicines to release the exterior. They are indicated for exterior pattern. Based on the property and actions, these medicines are further categorized into two types: medicines to remove wind and dissipate cold and medicines to remove wind and clear heat."
* #2250 #2602 #2685 "辛溫解表藥；發散風寒藥" "Medicines that are pungent in flavour and warm in property. They mainly act to remove wind and dissipate cold and are indicated for wind cold exterior pattern. Some medicines can also remove wind, alleviate itching, relieve pain, stop coughing, promote urination to resolve oedema, and heal sores/ulcers. These medicines can be used for wind cold-induced itchy rashes, cough, oedema, sores/ulcers, and bi-impediment due to wind and dampness."
* #2250 #2602 #2686 "辛涼解表藥；發散風熱藥" "Medicines that are pungent in flavour and cool in property. They mainly act to remove wind and clear heat and are indicated for wind heat exterior pattern. Some medicines can also clear the head/ eyes, benefit the throat, promote skin eruption and stop coughing. These medicines can be used for wind heat-induced red, swollen and painful eyes, red, swollen throat, inhibited skin eruption, and cough due to wind heat."
* #2250 #2602 #2687 "清熱藥" "Medicines to clear interior heat. They are indicated for interior heat pattern. Based on the property and actions, these medicines are further categorized into five types: medicines to clear heat/fire, medicines to clear heat and dry dampness, medicines to clear heat and cool blood, and medicines to clear deficiency heat."
* #2250 #2602 #2688 "清熱瀉火藥" "Medicines that are bitter or sweet in flavour and cold in nature. They are mainly indicated for excess heat in the qi phase and fire heat in the zang–fu organs, including lung heat, stomach fire, heart fire or liver fire."
* #2250 #2602 #2689 "清熱燥濕藥" "Medicines that are bitter in flavour and cold in property. They mainly act to clear heat and dry dampness and are indicated for damp heat pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2690 "清熱涼血藥" "Medicines that are sweet, bitter or salty in flavour and cold in property. They are mainly indicated for excess heat in the Ying nutrients and blood phases. Some medicines can also nourish yin and engender fluids. These medicines can be used for yin deficiency causing damage to fluids."
* #2250 #2602 #2691 "清熱解毒藥" "Medicines that are bitter in flavour and cold in property. They mainly act to clear heat and remove toxins and are therefore indicated for conditions due to toxic heat. Some medicines can also be used for snake bite or cancer."
* #2250 #2602 #2692 "清虛熱藥" "Medicine that are cold/cool in property. They mainly act to clear deficiency heat and alleviate bone-steaming sensation. They are indicated for internal heat due to yin deficiency."
* #2250 #2602 #2693 "瀉下藥" "Medicines to produce bowel movements. They are indicated for constipation or difficulty passing of stools. Based on the property and actions, these medicines are further categorized into three types: draining purgative medicines, moistening purgative medicines and drastic purgative medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2694 "攻下藥" "Medicines that are bitter in flavour and cold in property. They have strong effect in promoting bowel movements and clearing fire/heat. These medicines are indicated for constipation due to excess heat and retention of dry faeces."
* #2250 #2602 #2695 "潤下藥" "Medicines that are sweet in flavour and oily in quality. They mainly act to moisten the intestine, soften faeces and promote bowel movements. They are indicated for constipation due to exhaustion of fluids, yin deficiency or blood deficiency resulting from ageing, a weak constitution, chronic disease and childbirth."
* #2250 #2602 #2696 "峻下逐水藥" "Medicines that are bitter in flavour and cold in property. These medicines often have a drastic effect and can cause severe diarrhoea and relieve retained water. They are indicated for oedema, pleural effusion, ascites, or panting due to phlegm- fluid retention."
* #2250 #2602 #2697 "祛風濕藥" "Medicines to eliminate wind, resolve dampness and alleviate pain. They are indicated for bi-impediment patterns. Based on the bi-impediment category and medicinal property, these medicines are further categorized into three types: medicines to remove wind and resolve dampness; medicines to remove wind, clear heat and resolve dampness; and medicines to remove wind, resolve dampness and strengthen tendons and bones."
* #2250 #2602 #2698 "化濕藥；芳香化濕藥" "Medicines that are aromatic in smell and dry in property. They mainly act to transform dampness and benefit the spleen. These medicines are indicated for dampness affecting the spleen and stomach."
* #2250 #2602 #2699 "利水滲濕藥" "Medicines to unblock the water passage and drain water dampness. They are indicated for internal retention of water dampness."
* #2250 #2602 #2700 "溫裡藥；祛寒藥" "Medicines to warm the interior and dissipate cold. They are indicated for interior cold pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2701 "理氣藥" "Medicines to regulate qi activity. They are indicated for qi stagnation and adverse flow of qi."
* #2250 #2602 #2702 "消食藥" "Medicines to promote digestion and transform food stagnation. They are indicated for disorders due to food stagnation."
* #2250 #2602 #2703 "驅蟲藥" "Medicines to expel or kill parasites. They are indicated for intestinal parasites."
* #2250 #2602 #2704 "止血藥" "Medicines to stop bleeding. They are indicated for internal or external bleeding. Based on the property and functions, these medicines are further categorized into four types: medicines to cool blood and stop bleeding, medicines to transform stasis and stop bleeding, medicines to astringe and stop bleeding and medicines to warm meridians and stop bleeding."
* #2250 #2602 #2705 "涼血止血藥" "Medicines that are sweet and bitter in flavour and cold and cool in property. They mainly act to clear pathogenic heat in the blood phase. These medicines are indicated for bleeding due to heat- induced reckless blood flow."
* #2250 #2602 #2706 "化瘀止血藥；活血止血藥" "Medicines to stop bleeding and circulate blood to transform stasis. They act to stop bleeding but do not cause stasis; and circulate blood but do not cause bleeding. These medicines are indicated for haemorrhagic conditions due to stagnant blood or blood failing to circulate within the vessels."
* #2250 #2602 #2707 "收斂止血藥" "Medicines that are astringent and neutral in property. They mainly act to astringe and stop bleeding. These medicines are widely used for all kinds of bleeding."
* #2250 #2602 #2708 "溫經止血藥" "Medicines that are warm/heat in property. They mainly act to warm the interior, dissipate cold, warm meridians and stop bleeding. These medicines are indicated for bleeding due to deficiency cold."
* #2250 #2602 #2709 "活血祛瘀藥" "Medicines to circulate blood and resolve stasis. They are indicated for blood stasis. Based on the functions and clinical application, these medicines are further categorized into four types: medicines to circulate blood and stop pain; medicines to circulate blood and resolve stasis; medicines to circulate blood and heal the wound; and medicines to break blood and resolve masses."
* #2250 #2602 #2710 "化痰藥" "Medicines to transform or eliminate phlegm. Based on the property and functions, they are further categorized into two types: medicines to warm and transform cold phlegm; and medicines to clear heat and transform heat phlegm."
* #2250 #2602 #2711 "溫化寒痰藥" "Medicines that are pungent and bitter in flavour and warm and dry in property. They mainly act to warm the lung, eliminate cold, dry dampness and transform phlegm. These medicines are indicated for disorders due to cold phlegm or damp phlegm."
* #2250 #2602 #2712 "清化熱痰藥" "Medicines that are cold or cool in property. They mainly act to clear heat and transform phlegm. These medicines are indicated for disorders due to heat phlegm or dry phlegm."
* #2250 #2602 #2713 "止咳平喘藥" "Medicines to stop coughing and alleviate panting. These medicines enter the lung meridian and are indicated for coughing and panting."
* #2250 #2602 #2714 "安神藥" "Medicines to calm the mind. Based on the property and efficacy, these medicines are further categorized into two types: heavyweight medicines to tranquilize the mind; and medicines to nourish the heart mind."
* #2250 #2602 #2715 "重鎮安神藥" "Medicines (mostly minerals, fossil bones or shells) heavy in weight and downbearing in property. These medicines are indicated for excess mental uneasiness including palpitations, insomnia, convulsions, epilepsy or mania."
* #2250 #2602 #2716 "養心安神藥" "Medicines that are sweet in flavour, moist in quality and tonic or neutral in property. They mainly act to nourish the heart and tranquilize the mind. These medicines are indicated for mild or severe palpitations due to malnourishment of heart mind, and insomnia, poor memory and dream-disturbed sleep due to heart yin or heart blood deficiency."
* #2250 #2602 #2717 "平肝息風藥" "Medicines to soothe the liver and remove wind. They are indicated for hyperactivity of liver yang and internal stirring of liver wind. Based on their functions and indications, these medicines can be further categorized into two subtypes: medicines to soothe liver yang; and medicines to stop liver wind."
* #2250 #2602 #2718 "平降肝陽藥" "Medicines that are heavy in weight and cold in property. They mainly act to suppress or submerge yang. They are indicated for dizziness, headache or tinnitus due to hyperactivity of liver yang, and a red face and eyes, restlessness, and bitter mouth due to ascending of liver fire."
* #2250 #2602 #2719 "息風止痙藥" "Medicines that are cold, cool or neutral in property. They mainly act to soothe the liver, stop wind and resolve spasm/convulsions. They are indicated for dizziness, blurred vision, neck rigidity, tremor of limbs, spasms, epilepsy, convulsions, tetanus or opisthotonus due to internal stirring of liver wind."
* #2250 #2602 #2720 "開竅藥" "Medicines that are aromatic and pungent to open the orifices and refresh the mind. They are indicated for mental unconsciousness in block pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2721 "補虛藥" "Medicines to reinforce the body and prevent against diseases. They are indicated for deficiency pattern. Based on the property, functions and indications, these medicines are categorized into medicines to tonify qi, medicines to tonify yang, medicines to tonify blood and medicines to nourish yin."
* #2250 #2602 #2722 "補氣藥" "Medicines that are sweet in flavour and warm or neutral in property. They mainly act to tonify qi and are indicated for qi deficiency."
* #2250 #2602 #2723 "補血藥" "Medicines that are sweet in flavour and warm in property. They mainly act to tonify blood and are indicated for blood deficiency."
* #2250 #2602 #2724 "補陽藥" "Medicines that are sweet and pungent in flavour and warm in property. They mainly act to tonify yang qi and are indicated for yang deficiency."
* #2250 #2602 #2725 "補陰藥" "Medicines that are sweet in flavour and cold in property. They mainly act to nourish yin fluids and are indicated for yin deficiency (insufficiency of fluids)."
* #2250 #2602 #2726 "收澀藥；固澀藥" "Medicines to astringe and secure qi, blood, essence and body fluids. They are indicated for spontaneous sweating, night sweats, nocturnal emissions, enuresis, persistent leucorrhoea, uterine bleeding, chronic diarrhoea and chronic dysentery. Based on the property, efficacy and indications, astringent medicines are further divided into medicines to secure the exterior and stop sweating, medicines to astringe the lung and intestine and medicines to secure semen, astringe the urine and stop leucorrhoea."
* #2250 #2602 #2727 "固表止汗藥" "Medicines that are sweet in flavour and neutral in property. They mainly act to secure the exterior and stop sweating and are indicated for spontaneous sweating or night sweats."
* #2250 #2602 #2728 "斂肺澀腸藥" "Medicines that are sour and puckering in flavour and have astringent properties. They mainly act to astringe the lung and intestine, alleviate coughing/panting, and stop diarrhoea. These medicines are indicated for chronic coughing/ panting and persistent diarrhoea/dysentery."
* #2250 #2602 #2729 "固精縮尿止帶藥" "Medicines that are sour and puckering in flavour and astringent in property. They mainly act to secure semen, astringe urination and stop vaginal discharge. These medicines are mainly indicated for nocturnal emissions/spermatorrhoea, enuresis/profuse urine, or persistent vaginal discharge."
* #2250 #2602 #2730 "湧吐藥；催吐法" "Medicines to produce nausea and vomiting. They are indicated for food poisoning, indigestion and phlegm stagnation in the stomach or above the chest."
* #2250 #2602 #2731 "外用藥" "Medicines used for topical areas to remove toxins, resolve swelling, drain pus, regenerate tissue, heal wounds, kill insects and stop itching. They are indicated for sores, ulcers, carbuncles, scabies, traumatic injuries, snake bites or problems of the five sense organs. The method includes topical plaster, embrocating, fumigation, blow throat, nose and eye drops."
* #2250 #2602 #2732 "方劑類"
* #2250 #2602 #2733 "時方" "Formulas by physicians after Zhang Zhongjing in the Han Dynasty (220 to 202 BC)."
* #2250 #2602 #2734 "經方" "Formulas recorded in medical books before the Han dynasty, represented by Zhang Zhongjing."
* #2250 #2602 #2735 "驗方" "Effective formulas that have been demonstrated or confirmed by long clinical practice."
* #2250 #2602 #2736 "古方" "The formulas before the Qing Dynasty (1644–1911)."
* #2250 #2602 #2737 "秘方" "Effective and secret formulas."
* #2250 #2602 #2738 "湯頭；湯方；湯頭歌訣" "Oral herbal formulas to be decocted with water. Wang Ang, a Qing dynasty physician compiled a book named Tang Tou Ge Jue (Decoction in Rhymes) to make it easier for learners to memorize."
* #2250 #2602 #2739 "七方" "Seven formula types include major formulas, minor formulas, slow-acting formulas, fast-acting formulas, odd-numbered formulas, even- numbered formulas and compound formulas."
* #2250 #2602 #2740 "大方" "Formulas used for combined patterns upon contraction of excessive pathogenic factors. These include the following five aspects: ① drastic potency; ② many ingredients; ③ large doses; ④ take the large amount in one single dose; ⑤ indicated for major, severe disorders of the lower jiao."
* #2250 #2602 #2741 "小方" "Formulas used for one pattern alone upon contraction of mild pathogenic factors. These include the following three aspects: ① mild medical conditions; ② indicated for disorders of the upper jiao and take in small amount in frequent intervals; ③ fewer ingredients"
* #2250 #2602 #2742 "緩方；緩劑" "Formulas indicated for chronic conditions with a weak constitution. These include the following six aspects: ① many mutually-counterbalanced ingredients; ② use non-toxic ingredients to alleviate symptoms but do not cause damage to the anti-pathogenic qi; ③ use slow-acting ingredients; ⑤ use pills to remove pathogenic factors gradually; ⑥ use demulcent medicines to boost the immune system to help recovery."
* #2250 #2602 #2743 "急方" "Formulas indicated for emergency or critical conditions. These include the following four aspects: ① critical conditions that require immediate care; ② fast purging efficacy; ③ drastic potency; ④ directed at symptoms in acute conditions, such as Kai Guan San (Lockjaw- Relieving Powder) or Si Ni Tang (Frigid Extremities Decoction)."
* #2250 #2602 #2744 "奇方" "Formulas with one single ingredient or odd- numbered ingredients."
* #2250 #2602 #2745 "偶方" "Formulas with two or even-numbered ingredients."
* #2250 #2602 #2746 "複方；重方" "Formulas consisting of two or more set formulas. They are often used for complex conditions."
* #2250 #2602 #2747 "方劑學" "The study of the origin and evolution of formulas, treatment methods, composition principles, compatibility and clinical applications of Chinese herbal formulas."
* #2250 #2602 #2748 "八法" "The eight methods are categorized according to functions of the formulas. They are sweating , vomiting, draining downward , harmonizing , warming , clearing, reducing and tonifying."
* #2250 #2602 #2749 "汗法" "A method to induce perspiration, facilitate interaction between the Ying nutrients and Wei- defence, and disperse lung qi. It helps to expel six exogenous pathogenic factors through sweating and thus releases the exterior."
* #2250 #2602 #2750 "吐法" "A method to induce vomiting to fast remove phlegm, indigested food and poisons from the throat, thoracic diaphragm or stomach."
* #2250 #2602 #2751 "下法" "A method to induce bowel movements to cleanse or eliminate indigested food, dry faeces, cold accumulation, stagnant blood, phlegm, and water retention. It allows for the removal of pathogenic factors out of the body."
* #2250 #2602 #2752 "和法" "A method to moderate different parts of the body to expel pathogenic factors and regulate functions of the zang–fu organs. It helps to achieve yin- yang or interior–exterior balance by releasing the Shaoyang pattern when the pathogen lies in between the interior and exterior of the body and by harmonizing zang–fu organs."
* #2250 #2602 #2753 "溫法" "A method to warm the interior to eliminate pathogenic cold."
* #2250 #2602 #2754 "消法" "A method to promote digestion, circulate qi, move blood, transform phlegm, drain water retention and kill parasites."
* #2250 #2602 #2755 "清法" "A method to clear heat, reduce fire, remove toxins and cool blood to eliminate pathogenic heat in the interior."
* #2250 #2602 #2756 "補法" "A method to reinforce qi, blood, yin and yang of the body. Indicated for deficiency of zang–fu organs, qi, blood, yin and yang."
* #2250 #2602 #2757 "十劑" "A categorization method according to functions of the formula. The 10 types are dispersing formula, unblocking/unclogging formula, tonifying formula, purgative formula, formula consisting of light-weighted medicines, formula consisting of heavy-weighted medicines, lubricative formula, astringent formula, dry formula and moistening formula."
* #2250 #2602 #2758 "宣劑" "Formulas that disperse congestion and relieve stagnation."
* #2250 #2602 #2759 "通劑" "Formulas that move qi and circulate blood."
* #2250 #2602 #2760 "補劑" "Formulas that nourish and tonify qi, blood, yin and yang of the five zang organs."
* #2250 #2602 #2761 "泄劑；瀉劑" "Formulas that promote bowel movements and unclog the fu organs."
* #2250 #2602 #2762 "輕劑" "Formulas that remove pathogenic factors out of the exterior."
* #2250 #2602 #2763 "重劑" "Formulas that tranquilize and calm the mind."
* #2250 #2602 #2764 "澀劑；固澀劑" "Formulas that prevent qi, blood, essence and body fluids from exhaustion and collapse."
* #2250 #2602 #2765 "滑劑" "Formulas that diminish obstruction."
* #2250 #2602 #2766 "燥劑" "Formulas that dry dampness."
* #2250 #2602 #2767 "濕劑" "Formulas that moisten or nourish the body."
* #2250 #2602 #2768 "君藥；主藥" "Ingredient(s) that provide a dominant curative action on the main pattern/disorder or primary symptoms."
* #2250 #2602 #2769 "臣藥；輔藥" "① Ingredient(s) to help strengthen the action of the monarch medicines on main pattern or primary symptoms; ②ingredient(s) in a formula that provide a principal curative action for associated conditions or patterns."
* #2250 #2602 #2770 "佐藥" "Ingredient(s) to help the monarch and minister medicines treat principal or associated patterns; to counterbalance the toxicity or drastic actions of the monarch or minister medicines; or has/ have the opposite flavour and property with the monarch medicines. They are further categorized into facilitating assistant medicines, counterbalancing assistant medicines and counteracting assistant medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2771 "佐助藥" "Ingredient(s) to help facilitate the action of the monarch and minister medicines on main or associated patterns."
* #2250 #2602 #2772 "佐製藥" "Ingredient(s) to eliminate or moderate the toxicity and drastic actions of the monarch and minister medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2773 "反佐藥" "Ingredient(s) with a property opposite to and a therapeutic effect complementary to the monarch medicine(s)."
* #2250 #2602 #2774 "使藥" "Ingredient(s) to coordinate the rest of the ingredients or direct action to the affected meridians or sites. They are further categorized into coordinating medicines and meridian- directing medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2775 "調和藥" "The guide ingredient(s) to coordinate the rest of the ingredients in a formula."
* #2250 #2602 #2776 "劑型" "The form of a prepared medicine designed for medical treatment or prevention."
* #2250 #2602 #2777 "湯劑；煎劑；湯液" "A liquid preparation form by boiling or soaking the ingredients in water, and taken after the sediment is removed."
* #2250 #2602 #2778 "丸劑" "A solid globular mass made of finely powdered medicines with a suitable excipient or binder. It can be further categorized into honeyed pills, water pills, water-honeyed pills, paste pills, concentration pills, wax-wrapped pills, and mini pills."
* #2250 #2602 #2779 "蜜丸" "Pills made from fine medicinal powder and uses honey as the excipient."
* #2250 #2602 #2780 "水丸" "Pills made from fine medicinal powder and uses water (alternatively, alcohol, vinegar, honeyed water or medicinal juice) as excipient."
* #2250 #2602 #2781 "糊丸" "Pills made from fine medicinal powder and uses rice paste, flour paste or medicated leaven paste as the excipient."
* #2250 #2602 #2782 "濃縮丸" "Pills made from concentrated paste of medicinal decoction coupled with other medicinal powder through drying, grinding and mixing with water, honey or medicinal juice."
* #2250 #2602 #2783 "蠟丸" "Pills made from fine medicinal powder and uses beeswax as the excipient."
* #2250 #2602 #2784 "微丸" "Pills with a diameter of less than 2.5 mm."
* #2250 #2602 #2785 "散劑" "A powder-like preparation made from a mixture of one or more herbs."
* #2250 #2602 #2786 "膏劑" "A medicated paste is made from decocted medicines with water or vegetable oil. It can be further categorized into fluid extract, extractum, ointment and plaster."
* #2250 #2602 #2787 "煎膏；膏滋；膏方" "A semi-liquid form made from condensed medicinal decoction and processed honey/sugar."
* #2250 #2602 #2788 "流浸膏" "A semi-liquid medicated preparation containing the active constituents with appropriate solvents."
* #2250 #2602 #2789 "浸膏" "A concentrated medicated preparation obtained by extracting the active constituents with appropriate solvents."
* #2250 #2602 #2790 "軟膏；藥膏" "A viscous, semi-solid preparation for external application. It is made from fine medicinal powder and ointment bases."
* #2250 #2602 #2791 "硬膏；膏藥；薄貼" "A solid medicated dressing for external application. Often consists of medicines, edible vegetable oil and red lead."
* #2250 #2602 #2792 "顆粒劑；沖劑" "Granulated preparations made by herbal extracts and an excipient or fine medicinal powder."
* #2250 #2602 #2793 "酒劑" "A liquid form of herbal extracts prepared with distilled liquour."
* #2250 #2602 #2794 "茶劑" "Preparations made from the infusion or decoction of herbs or herbal extracts."
* #2250 #2602 #2795 "錠劑" "A solid form (shaped like a spindle, cylinder or bar) of fine medicinal powder or coupled with adhesive agents."
* #2250 #2602 #2796 "條劑" "An external dosage form of fine medicinal powder mixed with mulberry paper."
* #2250 #2602 #2797 "線劑" "A dried external dosage form of soaked and decocted silk or cotton thread."
* #2250 #2602 #2798 "灸劑" "External dosage form of moxa wool or moxa sticks to apply to certain body parts or acupuncture points."
* #2250 #2602 #2799 "栓劑" "A solid dosage form made from fine medicinal powder and bases. It is inserted into the rectum/ vagina, where it dissolves or melts and exerts local or systemic effects."
* #2250 #2602 #2800 "平旦服" "To take the medicine in the morning on an empty stomach."
* #2250 #2602 #2801 "熱服" "A method to take the decoction when it is hot."
* #2250 #2602 #2802 "溫服" "A method to take the decoction when it is neither cold nor hot."
* #2250 #2602 #2803 "冷服" "A method to take the decoction when it becomes cold."
* #2250 #2602 #2804 "噙化；含化" "A method to melt the medicine in the mouth."
* #2250 #2602 #2805 "頓服" "A method to take the decoction all in one single dose."
* #2250 #2602 #2806 "分服" "A method to take the medicine in divided times."
* #2250 #2602 #2807 "頻服" "A method to take the decoction in small portions at frequent intervals."
* #2250 #2602 #2808 "解表劑" "Formulas to induce sweating, relieve muscle soreness, and/or promote the eruption of measles. They are indicated for exterior pattern. These formulas are categorized into three types— formulas to release the exterior with pungent, warm medicines, formulas to release the exterior with pungent, cool medicines and formulas to reinforce healthy qi and release the exterior."
* #2250 #2602 #2809 "辛溫解表劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of pungent, warm and dispersing medicines. They are indicated for wind cold exterior pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2810 "分經論治" "These formulas are often used for common cold, headache, and perspiration. A representative formula is Jiu Wei Qiang Huo Tang (Nine Ingredients Notopterygium Decoction)."
* #2250 #2602 #2811 "辛涼解表劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of pungent, cool and dispersing medicines. They are indicated for exterior wind heat pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2812 "扶正解表劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to release the exterior and reinforce the body. They are indicated for exterior pattern in individuals with a weak constitution."
* #2250 #2602 #2813 "瀉下劑" "Formulas to promote bowel movements, remove food stagnation in the stomach and intestines, or resolve water retention. They are indicated for excessive interior pattern. These formulas are categorized into four types: formulas to drain downward with cold-property medicines; formulas to drain downward with warm-property medicines; formulas to drain downward with moistening medicines; and formulas to drain downward with water-expelling medicines."
* #2250 #2602 #2814 "寒下劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of cold-property ingredients. They are indicated for interior excessive heat accumulation pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2815 "釜底抽薪" "Take away firewood from under the cauldron” s a metaphorical expression for the method of ischarging heat with purgation. These formulas ct to purge excess heat. A representative formula s Da Cheng Qi Tang (Major Purgative Decoction)."
* #2250 #2602 #2816 "溫下劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of warm, pungent medicines to warm yang and dissipate cold, coupled with bitter, cold medicines to purge pathogenic factors. They are indicated for excessive interior cold pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2817 "潤下劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to nourish yin and purge the intestine. They are indicated for constipation due to intestinal dryness."
* #2250 #2602 #2818 "逐水劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of purgative medicines to resolve water retention. They are indicated for interior water retention."
* #2250 #2602 #2819 "攻補兼施劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to purge the intestine and tonify the body. They are indicated for interior excess and deficiency of healthy qi."
* #2250 #2602 #2820 "增水行舟" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to nourish yin and generate fluids. They are indicated for constipation due to heat in Yangming organ. A representative formula is Zeng Ye Tang (Fluid- Increasing Decoction)."
* #2250 #2602 #2821 "和解劑" "Formulas to harmonize and release Shaoyang, coordinate the liver and spleen, and regulate the stomach and intestines. They are used to treat pathogenic factor affecting the Shaoyang, disharmony between the liver and spleen and lack of coordination between the stomach and intestines. These formulas are categorized into three types: formulas to harmonize Shaoyang; formulas to harmonize the liver and spleen; and formulas to harmonize the stomach and intestines."
* #2250 #2602 #2822 "和解少陽劑" "Formulas that are indicated for cold damage affecting the Shaoyang (combined exterior and interior pattern)."
* #2250 #2602 #2823 "調和肝脾劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to soothe and soften the liver, regulate qi, nourish blood and strengthen the spleen. They are indicated for disharmony between the liver and spleen."
* #2250 #2602 #2824 "調和腸胃劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of pungent warm and bitter, cold medicines to disperse and down- regulate qi. They are indicated for disharmony between the stomach and intestines."
* #2250 #2602 #2825 "清熱劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of heat-clearing medicines. They act to clear fire/heat, cool blood and remove toxins. These formulas are categorized into six types: formulas to clear heat in the qi phase; formulas that clear heat in the Ying nutrients phase and cool blood; formulas to clear heat in both qi and blood; formulas to clear heat and remove toxins; formulas to clear heat in Zang– fu organs; and formulas to clear deficiency heat."
* #2250 #2602 #2826 "清氣分熱劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat. They are indicated for heat in the qi phase."
* #2250 #2602 #2827 "甘寒清熱" "Formulas that are indicated for exuberant heat damaging bodily fluids. They mainly consist of sweet and cold medicines: the sweet ones to nourish yin and regenerate fluids, and the cold/ cool ones to clear heat. A representative formula is Wu Zhi Yin (Five-Juice Beverage)."
* #2250 #2602 #2828 "清營涼血劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat and cool blood. They are indicated for heat entering the Ying nutrients and blood phases."
* #2250 #2602 #2829 "透熱轉氣" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat and remove toxins, coupled with medicines to clear heat in the qi phase. They are indicated for the early stage of heat entering the Ying nutrients phase in febrile diseases. A representative formula is Qing Ying Tang (Ying Phase Clearing Decoction)."
* #2250 #2602 #2830 "火鬱發之" "Formulas to expel stagnant heat/fire through dispersing, ascending and unblocking using light medicines. Representative formulas are Pu Ji Xiao Du Yin (Universal Relief Toxin-Removing Beverage) and Qing Wei San (Stomach-Clearing Powder)."
* #2250 #2602 #2831 "以瀉代清" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat and purge the intestine. They are indicated for interior heat. A representative formula is Liang Ge San (Diaphragm-Cooling Powder)."
* #2250 #2602 #2832 "清熱解毒劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat and remove toxins. They are indicated for pestilence, pathogenic warmth and toxic heat pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2833 "苦寒直折" "Formulas that are indicated for exuberant interior heat. These formulas mainly consist of bitter, cold medicines: the bitter ones to clear fire and the cold ones to clear heat. A representative formula is Huang Lian Jie Du Tang (Coptis Toxin-Resolving Decoction)."
* #2250 #2602 #2834 "氣血兩清劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat, cool blood and remove toxins. They are indicated for pestilence heat in both qi and blood."
* #2250 #2602 #2835 "清臟腑熱劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear internal heat. They are indicated for interior heat pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2836 "清虛熱劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear deficiency heat. They are indicated for deficiency heat."
* #2250 #2602 #2837 "先入後出" "Formulas that are used for later stage of febrile diseases with retention of pathogenic factors in the Yin phase. A representative formula is Qing Hao Bei Jie Tang (Sweet Wormwood and Turtle Shell Decoction). Bie Jia (Carapax Trionycis) guides Qing Hao (Herba Artemisiae Annuae) to the interior and Qing Hao guides Bie Jia out to the exterior. The combination of the two ingredients nourishes yin and clears internal heat."
* #2250 #2602 #2838 "祛暑劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear summer heat. They are indicated for summer heat- related problems. These formulas are categorized into four types: formulas to clear summer heat; formulas to clear summer heat and release the exterior; formulas to clear summer heat and resolve dampness; and formulas to clear summer heat and benefit qi."
* #2250 #2602 #2839 "祛暑清熱劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear summer heat. They are indicated for summer heat pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2840 "祛暑解表劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear summer heat, transform dampness or release the exterior. They are indicated for exposure to wind heat and summer heat damaging the spleen and stomach."
* #2250 #2602 #2841 "祛暑利濕劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear summer heat and resolve dampness. They are indicated for summer heat and dampness or downward flow of summer heat and dampness."
* #2250 #2602 #2842 "清暑益氣劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear summer heat, supplement qi, and nourish yin. They are indicated for summer heat damaging qi and bodily fluids."
* #2250 #2602 #2843 "溫裡劑" "Formulas to warm the interior, supplement yang, dispel cold, and unblock blood vessels. They are indicated for interior cold pattern. These formulas are categorized into three types: formulas to warm the middle jiao and remove cold; formulas to resuscitate yang to prevent collapse; and formulas to warm meridians and dispel cold."
* #2250 #2602 #2844 "溫中祛寒劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to warm the interior and remove cold. They are indicated for deficiency cold of the spleen and stomach."
* #2250 #2602 #2845 "回陽救逆劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to warm yang and prevent fainting. They are indicated for approaching collapse of yang qi."
* #2250 #2602 #2846 "溫經散寒劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to warm and unblock meridians and remove cold. They are indicated for joint pain due to cold retaining in meridians."
* #2250 #2602 #2847 "表裡雙解劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to release the exterior, warm the interior, and reinforce the body. They are indicated for pattern involving both the exterior and interior. These formulas are further categorized into three subtypes: exterior-releasing and interior-clearing formulas; exterior-releasing and interior-warming formulas; and exterior-releasing and interior- attacking formulas."
* #2250 #2602 #2848 "解表攻裡劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of exterior-releasing medicines and purgative medicines. They are indicated for retention of exogenous pathogens coupled with interior excess pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2849 "解表清裡劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to release the exterior and clear heat. They are indicated for retention of exogenous pathogens coupled with interior heat."
* #2250 #2602 #2850 "解表溫裡劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to release the exterior and warm the interior. They are indicated for retention of exogenous pathogens coupled with interior cold."
* #2250 #2602 #2851 "補益劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of tonic medicines. They are indicated for deficiency of qi, blood and yin and yang. These formulas are further categorized into six types: formulas to tonify qi; formulas to tonify blood; formulas to tonify qi and blood; formulas to nourish yin; formulas to tonify yang; and formulas to tonify yin and yang."
* #2250 #2602 #2852 "補氣劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of qi-tonifying medicines. They are indicated for qi deficiency pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2853 "甘溫除熱" "Formulas consisting of sweet, warm medicines. They are indicated for fever due to qi deficiency or yang deficiency. Representative formulas are Xiao Jian Zhong Tang (Minor Middle Jiao-Fortifying Decoction) and Bu Zhong Yi Qi Tang (Middle Jiao- Supplementing and Qi-Boosting Decoction)."
* #2250 #2602 #2854 "補血劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of blood-tonifying medicines. They are indicated for blood deficiency pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2855 "氣血雙補劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to tonify qi and blood. They are indicated for deficiency of both qi and blood."
* #2250 #2602 #2856 "補陰劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of yin-nourishing medicines. They are indicated for yin deficiency pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2857 "陽中求陰" "A method to combine a small amount of yang- tonifying medicines for kidney yin deficiency. This method enhances the effect of nourishing yin. The representative formula is Zuo Gui Wan (Left- Restoring Pill)."
* #2250 #2602 #2858 "補陽劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of yang-tonifying medicines. They are indicated for yang deficiency pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2859 "陰中求陽" "Formulas to combine a small amount of yin- nourishing medicines for kidney yang deficiency. This method enhances the effect of warming yang. The representative formula is You Gui Wan (Right- Restoring Pill)."
* #2250 #2602 #2860 "陰陽並補" "Formulas consisting of yin-nourishing and yang- tonifying medicines. They are indicated for deficiency of yin and yang."
* #2250 #2602 #2861 "安神劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to calm the mind. Indicated for mental restlessness. These formulas are categorized into two types: formulas to calm the mind with heavy medicines; and formulas to tranquilize the mind by nourishing yin and blood."
* #2250 #2602 #2862 "重鎮安神劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines (heavy in weight) to calm the mind. They are indicated for hyperactivity of heart yang and heat disturbing the heart mind."
* #2250 #2602 #2863 "滋養安神劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to nourish and calm the mind. They are indicated for yin blood failing to nourish the heart mind."
* #2250 #2602 #2864 "開竅劑" "Formulas containing aromatic medicines. They are indicated for loss of consciousness and blockage of orifices. These formulas are categorized into two types: formulas to clear heat; and formulas to warm the interior and open the orifices."
* #2250 #2602 #2865 "涼開劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat and open the orifices. They are indicated for heat blockage pattern due to toxic heat entering the pericardium in febrile disease."
* #2250 #2602 #2866 "溫開劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to dispel cold and open the orifices. They are indicated for cold blockage pattern: cold, dampness and turbid phlegm blocking the mind or filthy turbidity obstructing the flow of qi."
* #2250 #2602 #2867 "固澀劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to astringe or secure qi, blood, essence and body fluids. They are indicated for loss of or abnormal leakage/discharge of qi, blood, essence and body fluids. These formulas are categorized into five types: formulas to secure the exterior and stop sweating; formulas to astringe the lung and stop coughing; formulas to astringe the large intestine and stop diarrhoea; secure the essence gate and stop nocturnal emissions/urinary emissions; and secure Chong and Ren meridians and stop uterine bleeding/leucorrhoea."
* #2250 #2602 #2868 "固表止汗劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to astringe and stop sweating. They are indicated for sweating disorders."
* #2250 #2602 #2869 "斂肺止咳劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to astringe the lung. They are indicated for lung deficiency pattern due to chronic coughing."
* #2250 #2602 #2870 "澀腸固脫劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to astringe the intestines and secure collapse. They are indicated for faecal incontinence or rectal prolapse."
* #2250 #2602 #2871 "澀精止遺劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to secure the semen and astringe urination. They are indicated for nocturnal emissions or enuresis."
* #2250 #2602 #2872 "固崩止帶劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to stop bleeding and vaginal discharge. They are indicated for metrorrhagia, metrostaxis or profuse vaginal discharge."
* #2250 #2602 #2873 "理氣劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to move or down-regulate qi. They are indicated for qi stagnation or adverse ascending of qi. These formulas are categorized into two types: formulas to circulate qi; and formulas to down-regulate qi."
* #2250 #2602 #2874 "行氣劑" "Formulas that act to unblock qi activities and are often indicated for liver or spleen/stomach qi stagnation."
* #2250 #2602 #2875 "降氣劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to make the qi descend. These formulas are indicated for adverse ascending of qi."
* #2250 #2602 #2876 "理血劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of blood-regulating medicines. They are indicated for blood stasis or haemorrhage. These formulas are subdivided into two types: formulas to circulate blood and resolve stasis; and formulas to stop bleeding."
* #2250 #2602 #2877 "活血祛瘀劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of blood-circulating medicines. They are indicated for blood stasis pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2878 "剛柔相濟" "Formulas that combined dry, pungent and aromatic medicines with soft, sweet and moistening medicines. A representative formula is Huang Tu Tang (Yellow Earth Decoction)."
* #2250 #2602 #2879 "止血劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of haemostatic drugs. They are indicated for bleeding pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2880 "治風劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of wind-removing or wind-extinguishing medicines. They are indicated for external or internal wind."
* #2250 #2602 #2881 "疏散外風劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of pungent medicines to remove external wind. They are indicated for external wind pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2882 "平熄內風劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to soothe the liver and extinguish wind. They are indicated for internal stirring of liver wind."
* #2250 #2602 #2883 "治燥劑" "Formulas that act to dispel external dryness with pungent, light-weight ingredients or eliminate internal dryness with sweet, cold and yin-nourishing ingredients. These formulas are subdivided into two types: formulas to dispel external dryness; and formulas to nourish yin and moisten internal dryness."
* #2250 #2602 #2884 "輕宣潤燥劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of pungent medicines light in weight and dispersing in function. They are indicated for external dryness pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2885 "滋陰潤燥劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines (sweet in flavour and cool in property) to moisten dryness. They are indicated for internal dryness pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2886 "祛濕劑" "Formulas that act to resolve water retention and relieve stranguria are indicated for water dampness pattern. These formulas are categorized into five types: formulas to dry dampness and harmonize the stomach; formulas to clear heat and eliminate dampness; formulas to promote urination and drain dampness; formulas to warm and transform cold dampness; and formulas to remove wind and transform dampness."
* #2250 #2602 #2887 "燥濕和胃劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to dry dampness and harmonize the stomach. They are indicated for dampness affecting the spleen and stomach or disharmony of stomach qi."
* #2250 #2602 #2888 "清熱祛濕劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat and resolve dampness. They are indicated for damp heat pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2889 "利水滲濕劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines (sweet or bland in flavour) to drain dampness through urination. They are indicated for water dampness pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2890 "溫化水濕劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to warm yang and transform water and dampness. They are indicated for cold dampness pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2891 "祛風勝濕劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to remove wind and transform dampness. They are mainly indicated for wind dampness pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2892 "祛痰劑" "Formulas that are indicated for phlegm from all causes. These formulas are subdivided into five types: formulas to dry dampness and transform phlegm; formulas to clear heat and transform phlegm; formulas to moisten dryness and transform phlegm; formulas to warm and transform phlegm; and formulas to stop wind and transform phlegm."
* #2250 #2602 #2893 "燥濕化痰劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to dry dampness and transform phlegm. They are indicated for damp phlegm pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2894 "清熱化痰劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to clear heat and transform phlegm. They are indicated for heat phlegm pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2895 "潤燥化痰劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to moisten the lung and transform phlegm. They are indicated for dry phlegm pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2896 "溫化寒痰劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to warm and transform cold phlegm. They are indicated for cold phlegm pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2897 "治風化痰劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to remove/extinguish wind and transform phlegm. They are indicated for wind phlegm pattern."
* #2250 #2602 #2898 "消導化積劑" "Formulas to promote digestion. They are indicated for food stagnation from different causes. These formulas are subdivided into two types: formulas to promote digestion to resolve stagnation; and formulas to strengthen the spleen to promote digestion."
* #2250 #2602 #2899 "消食導滯劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to promote digestion. They are indicated for poor digestion due to food stagnation."
* #2250 #2602 #2900 "消痞化積劑" "Formulas mainly consisting of medicines to circulate qi, transform stasis, soften hardness and eliminate masses. They are indicated for stuffiness, fullness, stagnation or paediatric malnutrition due to food stagnation damaging the spleen and stomach."
* #2250 #2602 #2901 "驅蟲劑" "Formulas to kill intestinal parasites/worms. They are indicated for intestinal parasites."
* #2250 #2602 #2902 "湧吐劑" "Formulas to induce vomiting, expectorate phlegm, undigested food and poison in the stomach. They are indicated for phlegm syncope, food stagnation and food poisoning."
* #2250 #2602 #2903 "治瘍劑" "Formulas to remove toxins, resolve swelling, expel toxins, regenerate tissues, and heal the wound."
* #2250 #2602 #2904 "中成藥" "Safe, effective and reliable Chinese medical preparations that have been tested by clinical use and manufactured according to standard technical procedures."
* #2250 #2905 "3.4 養生保健方法"
* #2250 #2906 "3.5 針灸類(含拔罐、刮痧）"
* #2250 #2906 #2907 "經絡腧穴類"
* #2250 #2906 #2908 "經絡" "Meridians and collaterals are pathways for qi and blood to flow throughout the body. They form a comprehensive network linking the tissues and internal organs into an organic whole."
* #2250 #2906 #2909 "十二經脈；十二正經" "The twelve meridians (i.e. the twelve regular meridians) are: the lung meridian; the large intestine meridian; the stomach meridian; the spleen meridian; the heart meridian; the small intestine meridian; the bladder meridian; the kidney meridian; the pericardium meridian; the Sanjiao meridian; the gallbladder meridian; and the liver meridian."
* #2250 #2906 #2910 "手太陰肺經" "The lung meridian pertains to the lung and connects with the large intestine. It mainly travels along the anterior border of the medial aspect of the arm."
* #2250 #2906 #2911 "手陽明大腸經" "The large intestine meridian pertains to the large intestine and connects with the lung. It mainly travels along the anterior border of the radial aspect of the arm, passes through the cheeks, enters the lower teeth, and then over the upper lip and terminates beside the opposite nostril."
* #2250 #2906 #2912 "足陽明胃經" "The stomach meridian pertains to the stomach and connects with the spleen. It starts directly below the pupil between the eyeball and the infraorbital ridge. Running downward along the lateral side of the nose, to the lateral corner of the mouth. Curving posterior to the anterior angle of the mandible. Then it travels to the posterior aspect of the mandible ascending in front of the ear and following the anterior hairline, it reaches the forehead. It then runs along the throat and enters the supraclavicular fossa. The straight line of the meridian separates the supraclavicular fossa and runs downward along the middle mammillary line. It travels to the side of the umbilicus and descends to the inguinal groove. Running downward it travels along the anterior aspect of the thigh and reaches the knee. From there is continues further down along the anterior border of the lateral aspect of the tibia to the dorsum of the foot and reaches the lateral side of the tip of the second toe."
* #2250 #2906 #2913 "足太陰脾經" "The spleen meridian pertains to the spleen and connects with the stomach. It mainly travels along the anterior border of the medial aspect of the leg and passes through the chest and abdomen."
* #2250 #2906 #2914 "手少陰心經" "The heart meridian pertains to the heart and connects with the small intestine. It mainly travels along the posterior border of the ulnar palmar aspect of the arm."
* #2250 #2906 #2915 "手太陽小腸經" "The small intestine meridian pertains to the small intestine and connects with the heart. It mainly travels along the posterior border of the dorsal ulnar aspect of the arm, passes through the shoulder and reaches the face."
* #2250 #2906 #2916 "足太陽膀胱經" "The bladder meridian pertains to the urinary bladder and connects with the kidney. It is mainly distributed over the head, back and along the back of the leg."
* #2250 #2906 #2917 "足少陰腎經" "The kidney meridian pertains to the kidney and connects with the urinary bladder. It mainly travels along the posterior border of the medial aspect of the leg and passes through the chest and abdomen, finally terminating at the root of the tongue."
* #2250 #2906 #2918 "手厥陰心包經" "The pericardium meridian pertains to the pericardium and connects with the Sanjiao. It mainly travels along the palmar aspect of the upper arm and moves towards the forearm running between the tendons of the m. palmaris longus and m. flexor carpi radialis."
* #2250 #2906 #2919 "手少陽三焦經" "The Sanjiao meridian pertains to Sanjiao and connects with the pericardium. It is mainly distributed along the dorsal aspect of the forearm between the radius and ulna and reaches the ears and face."
* #2250 #2906 #2920 "足少陽膽經" "The gallbladder meridian pertains to the gallbladder and connects with the liver. It is mainly distributed over the head, sides of the head, chest, and abdomen and descends down to the lateral aspect of the leg."
* #2250 #2906 #2921 "足厥陰肝經" "The liver meridian pertains to the liver and connects with the gallbladder. It mainly travels along the medial aspect of the leg, passes over the chest and abdomen, and its divergent meridian reaches the vertex."
* #2250 #2906 #2922 "手三陰經" "The three hand-yin meridians are lung meridian, heart meridian and pericardium meridian."
* #2250 #2906 #2923 "手三陽經" "The three hand-yang meridians are large intestine meridian, small intestine meridian and Sanjiao meridian."
* #2250 #2906 #2924 "足三陰經" "The three foot-yin meridians are spleen meridian, kidney meridian and liver meridian."
* #2250 #2906 #2925 "足三陽經" "The three foot-yang meridians are stomach meridian, bladder meridian and gallbladder meridian."
* #2250 #2906 #2926 "奇經八脈" "The eight extraordinary meridians are different from the twelve regular meridians with special distributions and functions. They are Du meridian, Ren meridian, Chong meridian, Dai meridian, Yinwei meridian, Yangwei meridian, Yinqiao meridian and Yangqiao meridian."
* #2250 #2906 #2927 "督脈" "The Du meridian is mainly distributed over the head, face and along the midline of the back."
* #2250 #2906 #2928 "任脈" "The Ren meridian is mainly distributed over the neck, face and along the midline of the chest and abdomen."
* #2250 #2906 #2929 "沖脈" "The Chong meridian is mainly distributed over the chest and face, inside the spine and along the first lateral line of the abdomen."
* #2250 #2906 #2930 "帶脈" "The Dai meridian is mainly distributed over the waist, subcostal area and abdomen. It encircles the waist like a girdle."
* #2250 #2906 #2931 "陰蹻脈" "The Yinqiao meridian is mainly distributed over the heel, the medial aspect of the leg, perineum, abdomen, chest, head, and eyes."
* #2250 #2906 #2932 "陽蹻脈" "The Yangqiao meridian is mainly distributed over the heel, lateral aspect of the leg, thigh, subcostal area, shoulder, head and eyes."
* #2250 #2906 #2933 "陰維脈" "The Yinwei meridian is mainly distributed over the medial aspect of the leg and thigh and ascends along the third lateral line of the abdomen. It reaches the throat and meets with the Ren meridian at Tiantu (Ren 22) and Lianquan (Ren 23)."
* #2250 #2906 #2934 "陽維脈" "The Yangwei meridian is mainly distributed below the external malleolus, the lateral aspect of the leg, and over the subcostal area, shoulder and sides of the head. It meets with the Du meridian at Fengfu (Du 16) and Yamen (Du 15)."
* #2250 #2906 #2935 "絡脈" "Collaterals are branches of meridians, including minute collaterals, blood collaterals and superficial collaterals."
* #2250 #2906 #2936 "十五絡脈" "The fifteen collaterals include the collaterals of the twelve meridians, the collaterals of the Du and Ren meridian as well as the major collateral of the spleen."
* #2250 #2906 #2937 "十六絡脈" "The sixteen collaterals include the above- mentioned fifteen collaterals plus the major collateral of the stomach."
* #2250 #2906 #2938 "脾之大絡" "The major collateral of the spleen begins from Dabao (SP 21), emerges at 3 cun below Yuanye (GB 22) and spreads over the chest and subcostal area."
* #2250 #2906 #2939 "胃之大絡" "The major collateral of the stomach begins from Xuli (the pulsation at the apex of the heart), passes through the diaphragm, connects with the lung and emerges from the left breast."
* #2250 #2906 #2940 "血絡" "Superficial vessels visible to the naked eye."
* #2250 #2906 #2941 "浮絡" "Collaterals on the surface of the body."
* #2250 #2906 #2942 "孫絡" "Extremely small collaterals."
* #2250 #2906 #2943 "十二經別；經別" "The twelve divergent meridians branch out from the twelve regular meridians and are deeper in the body. They function to strengthen the connection between internally-externally coupled meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2944 "十二經筋；經筋" "The twelve muscle regions are corresponding sites where the qi and blood of the twelve meridians to nourish the muscles and tendons. Their distributions are almost the same as that of the twelve meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2945 "十二皮部；皮部" "The twelve cutaneous regions are corresponding sites where the qi and blood of the twelve meridians to nourish the skin. They are generally known as the outward manifestations of the twelve meridians and collaterals."
* #2250 #2906 #2946 "根結" "The word“Gen”literally means root. It shares the similar meaning with“Ben”.The word“Jie” literally means knot. It shares the similar meaning with“Biao”(see Biao and Ben)."
* #2250 #2906 #2947 "標本" "The word“biao”literally means the tip of tree branches. Here it refers to the head, face and body trunk where meridian qi diffuses. The word“ben” literally means the trunk of a tree. Here it refers to the four limbs where meridian qi gathers."
* #2250 #2906 #2948 "氣街" "Qi streets are the pathways for meridian qi to flow over the head and body trunk. There is a total of four qi streets, respectively on the head, chest, abdomen and lower leg."
* #2250 #2906 #2949 "四海" "The four seas include the sea of marrow, sea of qi, sea of water and food, and sea of blood (sea of the twelve meridians)."
* #2250 #2906 #2950 "髓海" "The brain is known as“the sea of marrow”."
* #2250 #2906 #2951 "氣海" "The chest is known as“the sea of qi”."
* #2250 #2906 #2952 "水穀之海" "The stomach is known as“the sea of water and food”."
* #2250 #2906 #2953 "血海" "The Chong meridian is known as“the sea of blood (sea of the twelve meridians)”."
* #2250 #2906 #2954 "陽脈之海" "The Du meridian governs meridian qi of all yang meridians and is therefore known as“the sea of yang meridians”."
* #2250 #2906 #2955 "陰脈之海" "The Ren meridian governs meridian qi of all yin meridians and is therefore known as“the sea of yin meridians”."
* #2250 #2906 #2956 "一源三歧" "The Du, Ren and Chong meridians all arise from a common origin:“baozhong”(the lower abdomen where primordial kidney qi gathers), emerge out of the perineum, and then travels along their own pathways."
* #2250 #2906 #2957 "循經感傳" "Sensations radiating along the pathways of meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2958 "經絡現象" "Specific disturbance of sensation, transmission of sensation and visible changes in skin colour and quality along the pathways of meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2959 "肺系" "The trachea and throat. The word“xi”means connecting tissues or structures."
* #2250 #2906 #2960 "目系" "The posterior intraocular structures connecting with the brain."
* #2250 #2906 #2961 "心系" "The structures connecting with the heart, such as blood vessels."
* #2250 #2906 #2962 "赤白肉際" "The boundary between the palmar aspect and dorsal aspect of the hands and feet. The skin on the palm aspect appears white, whereas the skin on the dorsal aspect appears red or brown. It is often used as an anatomical landmark to locate certain points."
* #2250 #2906 #2963 "關闔樞" "Three objects of a door are used figuratively to express the functions of the three yang and three yin meridians. Of the three yang meridians, Taiyang is compared to the bolt, Yangming to the plank and Shaoyang to the hinge. Of the three yin meridians, Taiyin is compared to the bolt, Jueyin to the plank and Shaoyin to the hinge."
* #2250 #2906 #2964 "腧穴" "The specific sites where the qi and blood of the zang–fu organs and meridians are transported to the surface of the body. They are pathological reaction points and, at the same time, acupuncture stimulation points."
* #2250 #2906 #2965 "經穴" "Acupuncture points of the fourteen meridians (i.e. twelve regular meridians + Du meridian + Ren meridian)."
* #2250 #2906 #2966 "經外奇穴；奇穴" "The points that are not contained in the above- mentioned fourteen meridians; however, these points have specific names and locations."
* #2250 #2906 #2967 "阿是穴；天應穴" "The points of sensitivity or tenderness. They are neither meridians points nor extra points, and they do not have specific names or locations."
* #2250 #2906 #2968 "特定穴" "The meridians points that have special names and functions."
* #2250 #2906 #2969 "五輸穴" "A collective term of the Jing-well, Ying-spring, Shu-stream, Jing-river and He-sea points. They are all located distal to, or at the elbow and knee joints."
* #2250 #2906 #2970 "井穴" "The point at which the qi rises is known as the Jing-well. Most Jing-well points are located on the fingers or toes."
* #2250 #2906 #2971 "滎穴" "The point at which the qi glides is known as the Ying-spring. The Ying-spring points are generally located distal to the metacarpophalangeal or metatarsophalangeal joints."
* #2250 #2906 #2972 "輸穴" "The point at which the qi pours through is known as the Shu-stream. The Shu-stream points are generally located proximal to the metacarpophalangeal joints or metatarsophalangeal joints."
* #2250 #2906 #2973 "經穴" "The point at which the qi flows is known as the Jing-river. The Jing-river points are located around the joints of the wrists or ankles."
* #2250 #2906 #2974 "合穴" "The point at which the qi enters inwards is known as the He-sea. The He-sea points are located around the joints of the elbows or knees."
* #2250 #2906 #2975 "原穴" "The points where the Yuan-primordial qi pools."
* #2250 #2906 #2976 "絡穴" "The points where a meridian exits off from the main flow and connects with the Yin/Yang paired meridian."
* #2250 #2906 #2977 "郤穴" "The points where meridian qi is deeply converged."
* #2250 #2906 #2978 "背俞穴" "The points on the back where qi of the respective zang–fu organs is infused."
* #2250 #2906 #2979 "募穴" "The points on the chest and abdomen where qi of the respective zang–fu organs is infused."
* #2250 #2906 #2980 "八會穴" "The points that have particular effects in the treatment of disorders relating to the zang, fu, qi, blood, tendon, vessels, bones and marrow."
* #2250 #2906 #2981 "八脈交會穴" "The points where qi of the twelve regular meridians meets the eight extraordinary meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2982 "下合穴" "The points where qi of the six fu organs is infused into the three foot-yang meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2983 "交會穴" "The points intersected by two or more meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2984 "針灸配穴" "The selection of different points in acupuncture treatment."
* #2250 #2906 #2985 "近部取穴" "A method to choose points at or close to the affected body area."
* #2250 #2906 #2986 "遠部取穴" "A method to choose points away from the affected body area."
* #2250 #2906 #2987 "隨證取穴" "A method to choose points according to differentiated pattern."
* #2250 #2906 #2988 "本經配穴法" "A method to combine points along the affected meridian."
* #2250 #2906 #2989 "表裡（經）配穴法" "A method to combine points from the internally- externally connected meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2990 "同名經配穴法" "A method to combine points from one hand meridian and one foot meridian that share the same name."
* #2250 #2906 #2991 "上下配穴法" "A method to combine points on the upper and lower body."
* #2250 #2906 #2992 "前後配穴法" "A method to combine points on the back and front of the body."
* #2250 #2906 #2993 "左右配穴法" "A method to combine points on the left and right side of the body."
* #2250 #2906 #2994 "原絡配穴法" "A method to combine the Yuan-source and Luo- connecting points."
* #2250 #2906 #2995 "主客原絡配穴法" "A point combination method to use the Yuan- primary point of the affected meridian coupled with the Luo-connecting point of its interiorly- exteriorly connected meridian. This method is often employed to treat problems of the zang–fu organs and meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #2996 "俞募配穴法" "A method to combine the back-Shu and front-Mu points."
* #2250 #2906 #2997 "腧穴定位法" "The acupuncture points can be located according to the anatomical landmarks, proportional bone measurement, and finger measurement, etc."
* #2250 #2906 #2998 "骨度分寸法" "A method to use landmarks on the body surface, primarily joints, to measure the length and width of various parts of the body. Then the width or length is divided respectively into definite numbers of equal units as the standards for the proportional measurement."
* #2250 #2906 #2999 "手指同身寸" "A method to locate acupuncture points based on the finger sizes of the patient."
* #2250 #2906 #3000 "拇指同身寸" "A method to take the width of the interphalangeal joint of the thumb as 1 cun."
* #2250 #2906 #3001 "中指同身寸" "A method to take the distance between the ends of the two radial creases of the interphalangeal joints of the middle finger as 1 cun when the middle finger is flexed."
* #2250 #2906 #3002 "橫指同身寸；一夫法" "A method to take the width of the patients’ four fingers (index, middle, ring and little) close together at the level of the dorsal skin crease of the proximal interphalangeal joint of the middle finger as three cun. It is also known as“Yi Fu Fa”in Chinese."
* #2250 #2906 #3003 "針刺類"
* #2250 #2906 #3004 "針尖" "The sharpest part of a needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3005 "針柄" "The part of the needle held by an acupuncturist."
* #2250 #2906 #3006 "針體（針身）" "The part between the tip and the handle."
* #2250 #2906 #3007 "針根" "The demarcation line between the handle and the body."
* #2250 #2906 #3008 "不銹鋼針" "Acupuncture needles made of stainless steel. It is now commonly used in clinical practice."
* #2250 #2906 #3009 "銀質針" "Acupuncture needles made of silver. They are often used in warming needle because of their good thermal and electrical conduction."
* #2250 #2906 #3010 "金質針" "Acupuncture needles made of gold."
* #2250 #2906 #3011 "針刺方法" "Basic acupuncture techniques."
* #2250 #2906 #3012 "毫針刺法" "Basic techniques in using filiform needles."
* #2250 #2906 #3013 "刺手" "The hand to hold and insert a needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3014 "押手" "The hand that presses the puncturing area with one or more fingers, to facilitate needle insertion."
* #2250 #2906 #3015 "雙手進針法" "A method to insert a needle into a point by using both the puncturing hand and pressing hand."
* #2250 #2906 #3016 "指切進針法" "A method to press the skin of puncturing area with the nail of thumb or index finger of the pressing hand and insert the needle against the fingernail with the needling hand."
* #2250 #2906 #3017 "夾持進針法" "A method to hold the lower part of the needle body with a piece of sterile cotton or gauze between the thumb and index finger of the pressing hand while inserting a needle. (Press the skin adjacent to the the needling site with the nail of thumb or index finger of the pressing hand, while inserting a needle against the fingernail with the needling hand)."
* #2250 #2906 #3018 "舒張進針法" "A method to stretch the skin around the puncturing area with the thumb and index finger (or the index and middle fingers) of the pressing hand while inserting a needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3019 "提捏進針法" "A method to pinch and lift the skin of the puncturing area with the thumb and index finger of the pressing hand while inserting a needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3020 "單手進針法" "A method to insert a needle into a point by using the puncturing hand alone."
* #2250 #2906 #3021 "管針進針法" "A method to insert a needle using a fine tube as a guide."
* #2250 #2906 #3022 "散刺法" "A needling technique to prick a point and its surrounding area."
* #2250 #2906 #3023 "挑刺法（挑治法）" "A needling technique to gently prick using a three- edged needle and let out a small amount of blood or tissue fluid."
* #2250 #2906 #3024 "點刺法" "A needling technique to prick superficially and then remove immediately. It is often used for Jing- well points or blood-letting."
* #2250 #2906 #3025 "刺絡法" "A needling technique to prick superficial collaterals using a three-edged or a (single- use, disposable) seven-star needle to let out appropriate amount of blood."
* #2250 #2906 #3026 "針刺角度" "The angle formed between the needle body and the skin while the needle is being inserted."
* #2250 #2906 #3027 "直刺" "The needle insertion at a 90° angle to the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3028 "斜刺" "The needle insertion at a 45° angle to the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3029 "平刺；橫刺，沿皮刺" "Needle insertion at a 15° angle to the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3030 "透刺" "A method to insert a needle from one point towards another point (or more points)."
* #2250 #2906 #3031 "行針；運針" "Manipulating the needle after insertion to produce the desired effect."
* #2250 #2906 #3032 "提插法" "A needling manipulation to lift and thrust a needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3033 "撚轉法" "A needling manipulation to twirl (turn) or rotate a needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3034 "循法" "A method to gently tap or massage the skin along the pathways of meridians to promote the flow of qi."
* #2250 #2906 #3035 "彈（柄）法" "A needling manipulation to flick the handle of the needle to promote the flow of qi."
* #2250 #2906 #3036 "刮（柄）法" "A needling manipulation to scrape the handle of the needle with fingernails to promote the flow of qi."
* #2250 #2906 #3037 "搖（柄）法" "A needling manipulation to shake the needle handle to promote the flow of qi."
* #2250 #2906 #3038 "飛法" "A needling manipulation to twist the needle handle with the thumb and index finger for a couple of times and release the fingers. The repeated twisting and releasing resembles a bird spreading its wings."
* #2250 #2906 #3039 "震顫法" "A needling manipulation to hold the handle of the needle and make a small-amplitude and rapid lifting and thrusting to cause the needle to vibrate up and down to promote the flow of qi."
* #2250 #2906 #3040 "得氣" "The arrival of qi is signalled by particular sensations felt by the patient and the practitioner. For the patient, it typically refers to soreness, numbness, distension and heaviness felt around the needle (but may move up or down along the meridian). For the practitioner, it refers to a gripping sensation or a feeling of pressure underneath the needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3041 "針感" "Typical sensations of soreness, numbness, distension and heaviness felt around the needle by the patient."
* #2250 #2906 #3042 "候氣" "A method to leave the needle in place for a while after insertion, without any manipulation."
* #2250 #2906 #3043 "催氣" "A method to manipulate the needle to stimulate the flow of qi."
* #2250 #2906 #3044 "氣至病所" "The effect of needling sensation reaching an affected area."
* #2250 #2906 #3045 "針刺補瀉" "Needling manipulations to reinforce healthy qi for deficiency pattern and to remove pathogenic factors for excess pattern."
* #2250 #2906 #3046 "撚轉補瀉" "Needling manipulations to reinforce or reduce according to the twirling/rotating direction, angle, frequency and duration."
* #2250 #2906 #3047 "提插補瀉" "Needling manipulations to reinforce or reduce according to the forcefulness, speed, amplitude and duration of the lifting and thrusting."
* #2250 #2906 #3048 "徐疾補瀉" "Needling manipulations to reinforce or reduce according to the slow or rapid needle insertion and withdrawal."
* #2250 #2906 #3049 "迎隨補瀉" "Needling manipulations to reinforce or reduce by going against or following the pathways of meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #3050 "呼吸補瀉" "Needling manipulations to reinforce or reduce by inserting or withdrawing the needle when the patient breathes in or out."
* #2250 #2906 #3051 "開闔補瀉" "Needling manipulations to reinforce or reduce by opening or closing the needle holes."
* #2250 #2906 #3052 "平補平瀉" "Needling manipulations to lift, thrust, or twirl evenly."
* #2250 #2906 #3053 "燒山火" "A complex reinforcing manipulation to induce local or general warming sensation. The point to be needled is divided into three levels: shallow (heaven), intermediate (man) and deep (earth). Insert the needle into the shallow level first and then down to the intermediate and deep levels, coupled with rapid thrusting and slow lifting for a total of nine times in each level. Repeat the procedure three times and leave the needle in the deep level."
* #2250 #2906 #3054 "透天涼" "A complex reducing manipulation to induce local or general cool sensation. The point to be needled is divided into three levels: shallow (heaven), intermediate (man) and deep (earth). Insert the needle to the deep level first and then lift to the intermediate and shallow levels, coupled with rapid lifting and slow thrusting for a total of six times in each level. Repeat the procedure three times and leave the needle in the shallow level."
* #2250 #2906 #3055 "子母補瀉法" "One of the reinforcing or reducing manipulations. Manipulations of reinforcing the mother or reducing the son are selected according to deficiency or excess of the zang–fu organs or meridian problems."
* #2250 #2906 #3056 "子午流注" "cial theory for point selection. The midnight- (Zi Wu) represents the time. The ebb-flow Zhu) refers to the circulation of qi and blood. theory believes the meridian qi and blood from Zi time (11:00 pm–1:00 am) to Wu time 0 am–1:00 pm) and then from the Wu time to i time. The yin qi becomes most abundant e Zi time and then begins to decline, and ang qi starts to increase and becomes most ant at the Wu time. Based on this theory, ive-Shu points located below the knee and joints should be selected according to the nated hours."
* #2250 #2906 #3057 "留針（置針）" "To retain a needle in place after the presence of needling sensation (arrival of qi)."
* #2250 #2906 #3058 "出針" "To remove a needle when the treatment is done."
* #2250 #2906 #3059 "針刺異常情況" "Acupuncture-related adverse events include fainting, stuck needle, bent needle and broken needle. These accidents are often caused by a weak constitution, nervousness, hunger, inappropriate body position or medical negligence."
* #2250 #2906 #3060 "暈針" "An adverse event during an acupuncture session: Patients may present with a pale complexion or experience dizziness, nausea and chest tightness. In severe cases, cold limbs, cold sweats, drop in blood pressure, syncope or shock may be present."
* #2250 #2906 #3061 "滯針" "Difficulty or impossible to rotate, lift or thrust a needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3062 "彎針" "The needle body becomes bent upon insertion or retention."
* #2250 #2906 #3063 "斷針" "The needle body is broken and the broken part is on or below the skin surface."
* #2250 #2906 #3064 "針刺血腫" "Localized swelling, distension and pain from bleeding in the tissue after needle withdrawal."
* #2250 #2906 #3065 "針刺性氣胸" "Pneumothorax induced by inappropriate acupuncture needling that punctures the lung."
* #2250 #2906 #3066 "微針療法" "A collective term for acupuncture in a specific local area, such as ear, scalp, or face, etc."
* #2250 #2906 #3067 "頭針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate specific lines on the scalp with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3068 "耳針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points on the ear with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3069 "耳穴" "Specific stimulation areas distributed on the auricles."
* #2250 #2906 #3070 "面針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points on the face with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3071 "眼針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points around the eye socket with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3072 "鼻針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points on the nose with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3073 "舌針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points on the tongue with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3074 "脊背針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points on the back with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3075 "手針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points on the hand with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3076 "足針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points on the foot with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3077 "腕踝針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate points on the wrist and ankle subcutaneously with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3078 "腹針療法" "A therapeutic method to stimulate specific abdominal points with an acupuncture needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3079 "第二掌骨側針法" "A method to stimulate the area lateral to the second metacarpal bone with an acupuncture needle. This method is based on micro-system theory that regards the second metacarpal bone as the whole body."
* #2250 #2906 #3080 "三棱針" "A thick needle with a round handle, a triangular body and a sharp tip similar to a lancet needle. It derives from the Sharp-edged Needle (one of the nine ancient needles)."
* #2250 #2906 #3081 "皮膚針" "Needling instrument composed of several short needles used for tapping the points, such as the plum-blossom needle or seven-star needle. These needles cannot be reused without sterilization."
* #2250 #2906 #3082 "梅花針" "A dermal needle that has a bundle of five short, embedded needles resembling a plum blossom. This needle cannot be reused without sterilization."
* #2250 #2906 #3083 "七星針" "A dermal needle that has seven short needles attached to the end of a handle in a cluster. This needle cannot be reused without sterilization."
* #2250 #2906 #3084 "皮內針" "A small needling instrument for embedding at a certain point for an extended period."
* #2250 #2906 #3085 "撳針" "An intradermal needle that is shaped like a thumbtack used for extended auricular therapy, approximately 0.2 mm–1.5 mm in length."
* #2250 #2906 #3086 "埋針療法；皮內針療法" "A therapy to insert an intradermal needle into the skin of the body surface (an acupuncture point) and fix with tape to retain for a period of time."
* #2250 #2906 #3087 "電針" "A therapeutic method to connect pulses of electrical current to inserted acupuncture needles."
* #2250 #2906 #3088 "穴位注射；水針" "A therapeutic method to inject fluid, sometimes medicated, into acupuncture points."
* #2250 #2906 #3089 "火針；燔針" "A therapeutic method to insert a hot needle to the affected area and remove immediately afterwards."
* #2250 #2906 #3090 "芒針" "A therapeutic method to puncture one point towards another one subcutaneously using a long, thready needle like wheat awn. It derives from the Long Needle (one of the nine ancient needles)."
* #2250 #2906 #3091 "鐳射針" "A therapeutic method to use a low-energy laser beam to stimulate acupuncture points."
* #2250 #2906 #3092 "蜂針療法" "A therapy to use the sting from a live bee (mechanical stimulation) and venom (pharmacological action) in the treatment of health conditions."
* #2250 #2906 #3093 "偽針；假針" "A research control method that simulates acupuncture needle insertion without actual insertion, preferably without any penetration that carries physiological response. The tip of a sham needle is blunt to prevent the actual penetration into the body, which effectively trick the patient into thinking they are receiving real acupuncture, such as the“Streitberger”needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3094 "割治" "A therapeutic method to incise the skin to squeeze out a small amount of fat."
* #2250 #2906 #3095 "穴位埋線" "A therapeutic method to implant catgut or surgical suture at a selected point."
* #2250 #2906 #3096 "砭石" "Pointed or wedge-shaped stones historically used to stimulate the surface of the body, drain pus or let blood out."
* #2250 #2906 #3097 "九針" "A collective term for the nine types of needles described in the Nei Jing (Yellow Emperor’ s Internal Classic), namely, shear needle, rounded needle, spoon-like needle, sharp-edged needle, round-sharp needle, sword-like needle, long needle, large needle and filiform needle."
* #2250 #2906 #3098 "鑱針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It has an arrow- shaped head and often used for superficial insertion in the treatment of heat pattern or skin conditions."
* #2250 #2906 #3099 "圓針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It has a cylindrical needle body and oval tip. It is often used for point massage in the treatment of muscle problems."
* #2250 #2906 #3100 "鍉針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It has a big, thick needle body and a slightly sharp round tip. It is often used for blood problems and heat pattern."
* #2250 #2906 #3101 "鋒針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It is also known as the three-edged needle. It is often used to prick subcutaneous veins or small vessels in the treatment of abscess, heat pattern, acute gastroenteritis, etc."
* #2250 #2906 #3102 "員利針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It has a round and sharp needle tip and is often used for abscess, bi-impediment and acute conditions."
* #2250 #2906 #3103 "鈹針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It has a (double- edged) sword-like needle tip and is often used to cut open the abscesses to drain pus and blood."
* #2250 #2906 #3104 "長針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It has a longer needle body (approximately 20–30 cm or longer) and is often used for deep insertion in the treatment of chronic rheumatism or sciatica."
* #2250 #2906 #3105 "大針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It has a thick needle body and a slightly round needle tip. It is often used to treat general oedema or abdominal masses."
* #2250 #2906 #3106 "毫針" "One of the nine ancient needles. It is now the most common needle in acupuncture treatment."
* #2250 #2906 #3107 "五刺" "A collective term for five ancient needling techniques, namely, half needling, leopard-spot needling, joint needling, Hegu needling, and Shu needling."
* #2250 #2906 #3108 "半刺" "A technique characterized by a shallow insertion and swift withdrawal. It is often used for lung- related problems."
* #2250 #2906 #3109 "豹文刺" "A technique characterized by pricking with a three-edged needle around the point, causing leopard spot-like haemorrhagic spots. It is often used for heart-related problems."
* #2250 #2906 #3110 "關刺" "A technique characterized by puncturing the tendon close to the joint without causing bleeding. It is often used for liver-related problems."
* #2250 #2906 #3111 "合穀刺" "A technique characterized by puncturing the muscles of the affected region with the needle going obliquely right and left, resembling the claws of a chicken. It is often used for spleen- related problems."
* #2250 #2906 #3112 "輸刺（五刺）" "A technique characterized by deep puncture directly to the bone. It is often used for kidney- related problems."
* #2250 #2906 #3113 "九刺" "A collective term for nine ancient needling techniques, namely, Shu needling, distant needling, meridian needling, collateral needling, intramuscular needling, great drainage needling, skin needling, cauterized needling and contralateral needling."
* #2250 #2906 #3114 "輸刺（九刺）" "A technique to puncture the Ying-spring point and Shu-stream point of the meridian involved and the back-Shu points of five zang organs."
* #2250 #2906 #3115 "遠道刺" "A technique to puncture points on the lower body (i.e. the lower He-sea points of the six fu organs) to treat problems of the upper body."
* #2250 #2906 #3116 "經刺" "A technique to acupuncture stasis/tenderness points along the pathways of meridians."
* #2250 #2906 #3117 "絡刺" "A technique to prick small vessels with a three- edged needle for bloodletting."
* #2250 #2906 #3118 "分刺" "A technique to puncture directly into the muscle."
* #2250 #2906 #3119 "大瀉刺" "A technique to incise with a sword-shaped needle to drain pus or eliminate stagnant blood, also known as lancing."
* #2250 #2906 #3120 "毛刺" "A technique to puncture shallowly into the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3121 "巨刺" "A technique to puncture points on the left side if the right side is affected, or vice versa."
* #2250 #2906 #3122 "繆刺" "A technique to conduct blood-letting on the left side if the right side is affected, or vice versa."
* #2250 #2906 #3123 "焠刺" "A technique to prick swiftly with a cauterized needle. Indicated for cold bi-impediment pattern."
* #2250 #2906 #3124 "十二刺" "A collective term for twelve ancient needling techniques, namely: coupled needling; successive trigger needling; relaxing needling; triple needling; quintuple needling; straight needling; Shu needling; short needling; superficial needling; yin needling; accompanied needling; and repeated shallow needling."
* #2250 #2906 #3125 "偶刺" "A technique to puncture the tenderness spots felt on the front (corresponding to the front-Mu point) and back (corresponding to the back-Shu point)."
* #2250 #2906 #3126 "報刺" "A technique to puncture migratory Ashi points: while needling directly at the tender point, searching for other tender points over the surrounding area and needling in succession."
* #2250 #2906 #3127 "恢刺" "A technique to insert the needle from the side of the tendon and then puncture the contracted muscle in different directions to induce relaxation."
* #2250 #2906 #3128 "齊刺" "A technique to insert one needle perpendicularly, coupled with two more needles by its sides. Used to remove cold in a small, deeper area."
* #2250 #2906 #3129 "揚刺" "A technique to insert one needle perpendicularly in the centre, coupled with four more needles anterior, posterior, right, and left to the perpendicular needle. Used to remove cold in a large, shallow area."
* #2250 #2906 #3130 "直針刺" "A technique to pinch the skin and insert the needle subcutaneously (The word“straight”here means towards the affected shallow area). Used to remove cold in the superficial area."
* #2250 #2906 #3131 "輸刺（十二刺）" "A technique to insert a needle perpendicularly to the deep area to wait for needling sensation and then slowly withdraw the needle when the needling sensation is present. Used to clear pathogenic heat."
* #2250 #2906 #3132 "短刺" "A technique to insert the needle slowly while gently shaking the needle, followed by mild rotation when the needle is close to the bone. Used to treat bone pain or tenderness."
* #2250 #2906 #3133 "浮刺" "A technique to puncture the superficial area obliquely. Used indicated for muscle pain with a cold sensation."
* #2250 #2906 #3134 "陰刺" "A technique to puncture bilateral Taixi (KI 3) (the Yuan-primordial point of the kidney meridian) simultaneously. Used to treat cold limbs."
* #2250 #2906 #3135 "傍針刺" "A technique to insert one needle perpendicularly, coupled with another one lateral to its side. Indicated for tenderness or pain with a fixed location."
* #2250 #2906 #3136 "贊刺" "A technique to repeatedly puncture the shallow areas to cause bloodletting. Often used for carbuncles or abscesses."
* #2250 #2906 #3137 "灸法類"
* #2250 #2906 #3138 "灸法" "A therapeutic method to apply burning mugwort (moxa) or other substances near or to particular points or areas of the body to relax, warm and tonify."
* #2250 #2906 #3139 "艾絨" "Processed moxa leaves that concentrates the white hairs on the underside of the leaf."
* #2250 #2906 #3140 "艾炷" "Cone-shaped mass made of moxa wool"
* #2250 #2906 #3141 "壯" "A dosage unit to count the number of moxa cones."
* #2250 #2906 #3142 "艾條" "A cigar-shaped roll of mugwort wrapped in cotton paper."
* #2250 #2906 #3143 "藥艾條" "Moxa sticks containing medicinal powder."
* #2250 #2906 #3144 "艾炷灸" "A method to place a moxa cone directly or indirectly to the selected area and then ignite."
* #2250 #2906 #3145 "直接灸" "A method to place a moxa cone directly on the selected area and then ignite to create a superficial burn."
* #2250 #2906 #3146 "瘢痕灸" "A method to place a small moxa cone directly on the skin, then ignite until the skin blisters and then scars after it heals."
* #2250 #2906 #3147 "化膿灸" "Please refer to scarring moxibustion."
* #2250 #2906 #3148 "無瘢痕灸" "A method to place a small moxa cone directly on the skin, then ignite and remove the burning moxa before the skin burns enough to scar."
* #2250 #2906 #3149 "麥粒灸" "A direct moxibustion method using a wheat grain size moxa cone."
* #2250 #2906 #3150 "間接灸；間隔灸，隔物灸" "A method to place some insulated materials between the moxa cone and the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3151 "隔薑灸" "A method to place a fresh ginger slice between the moxa cone and the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3152 "隔蒜灸" "A method to place a fresh garlic slice or mashed garlic between the moxa cone and the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3153 "隔鹽灸" "A method to place some salt (usually at Shenque (Ren 8)) between the moxa cone and the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3154 "隔附子餅灸" "A method to place a monkshood cake between the moxa cone and the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3155 "艾條灸" "A method of indirect moxibustion where an ignited moxa stick or roll passes over a selected area without directly touching the surface to warm the area."
* #2250 #2906 #3156 "懸灸；懸起灸" "An indirect moxibustion method to hold a moxa stick above the skin during the treatment."
* #2250 #2906 #3157 "溫和灸" "An indirect moxibustion method to keep the end of an ignited moxa stick at a fixed distance (2–3 cm) from the selected area, enabling a mild warm sensation."
* #2250 #2906 #3158 "雀啄灸" "An indirect moxibustion method to place an ignited moxa stick near the selected area and move it up and down like a pecking bird."
* #2250 #2906 #3159 "迴旋灸" "An indirect moxibustion method to keep the end of an ignited moxa stick at a fixed distance from the selected area and move it back and forth circularly."
* #2250 #2906 #3160 "實按灸" "An indirect moxibustion method to place several layers of cloth or cotton paper on the selected area and then press the ignited end of a moxa stick to allow the heat to penetrate the skin."
* #2250 #2906 #3161 "太乙神針" "An indirect moxibustion method to apply an ignited moxa stick to the selected area. The moxa stick is made of fine powder of Tan Xiang (Lignum Santali Albi), Shan Nai (Rhizoma Kaempferiae), Qiang Huo (Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii), Gui Zhi (Ramulus Cinnamomi), Mu Xiang (Radix Aucklandiae), Xiong Huang (Realgar), Bai Zhi (Radix Angelicae Dahuricae) and Xi Xin (Radix et Rhizoma Asari)."
* #2250 #2906 #3162 "雷火神針" "An indirect moxibustion method to apply an ignited moxa stick to the selected area. The moxa stick is made of fine powder of Chen Xiang (Lignum Aquilariae Resinatum), Mu Xiang (Radix Aucklandiae), Ru Xiang (Olibanum), Yin Chen (Herba Artemisiae Scopariae), Qiang Huo (Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii), Gan Jiang (Rhizoma Zingiberis), Chuan Shan Jia (Squama Manitis), and She Xiang(Moschus)."
* #2250 #2906 #3163 "溫針灸" "A method to fix a small section of moxa stick or cone onto the handle of an inserted needle prior to igniting."
* #2250 #2906 #3164 "溫灸器灸" "A method to put moxa wool or stick into a metal box or canister, ignite and then place the box or canister over the selected area."
* #2250 #2906 #3165 "燈火灸" "A method to apply a quick momentary touch to the selected area with an ignited oiled rush of Juncus."
* #2250 #2906 #3166 "天灸；藥物發泡法" "A method to apply irritants to the selected area to produce congestion and blistering."
* #2250 #2906 #3167 "穴位敷貼" "A method to apply warm or hot medicinal substances to acupuncture points."
* #2250 #2906 #3168 "拔罐類"
* #2250 #2906 #3169 "拔罐法" "A therapeutic method to create negative pressure by consuming the air inside the cup with fire or other methods and then place the cup on the skin over the selected area."
* #2250 #2906 #3170 "火罐法；火吸法" "A method to create negative pressure by introducing an ignited material inside the cup to consume the air, thereby enabling the cup to suck itself onto the skin. There are three different approaches: fire twinkling; fire throwing; and cotton sticking."
* #2250 #2906 #3171 "閃火法" "A method to ignite an alcoholic cotton ball held with a forceps, place it inside the cup, quickly turn it around in one to two circles, take it out immediately, and place the cup on the selected area."
* #2250 #2906 #3172 "投火法" "A method whereby an ignited paper is quickly flung into a cup to create a negative pressure on the selected area."
* #2250 #2906 #3173 "貼棉法" "A method to stick an alcoholic cotton ball on the inner wall (the lower 1/3), ignite the cotton ball and quickly place the cup over the selected area."
* #2250 #2906 #3174 "水罐法；水吸法" "A method to boil a cup in water to create negative pressure: to hold the boiled bamboo cup (the mouth facing downwards) with a forceps, cover the cup mouth with a towel to absorb the water in the cup, and then quickly place the cup over the selected area."
* #2250 #2906 #3175 "留罐法" "Leave the cup in place after suction."
* #2250 #2906 #3176 "走罐法" "A method to lubricate the cup mouth and selected area first and then slide the cup up and down while retaining its suction."
* #2250 #2906 #3177 "閃罐法" "A method to rapidly place and remove the cup repeatedly over the selected area until the local skin becomes red."
* #2250 #2906 #3178 "刺絡拔罐" "A method to prick the area to be treated with a three-edged needle or lancet and then suck out blood with a cup."
* #2250 #2906 #3179 "留針拔罐；針罐" "A method to apply cupping to the centre of the site where a needle is retained."
* #2250 #2906 #3180 "藥罐" "A boiling cup method to boil bamboo cups in medicinal liquid and then apply to the body."
* #2250 #2906 #3181 "刮痧療法" "A method to scrape the skin repeatedly using a soup spoon or other smooth-edged instruments (such as horned animal bones, water buffalo horn or jade). The skin is often lubricated with massage oil or water."
* #2250 #2906 #3182 "刮痧板" "Guasha tools are often made from buffalo horn, Bian-stone or jade."
* #2250 #3183 "3.6 推拿類"
* #2250 #3183 #3184 "推拿；按摩；按蹺；喬摩；案扤" "A Chinese medical therapy to apply manipulation to points or body areas for disease treatment and prevention as well as promoting health. Sometimes, patients are educated to exercise in addition to receiving treatment."
* #2250 #3183 #3185 "推拿手法" "Specific, standardized methods and movements to treat/prevent diseases and benefit health. Tuina manipulations are supposed to be lasting, forceful, even, soft and penetrating to the deeper tissue."
* #2250 #3183 #3186 "㨰法" "A tuina manipulation to stick to the area to be treated with the fifth metacarpophalangeal joint and conduct back-and-forth rolling using the hypothenar eminence and the ulnar side of the back of the hand."
* #2250 #3183 #3187 "滾法" "A tuina manipulation to clench a fist to allow the distal phalanx bone of the thumb to be inside the fist, stick to the area to be treated with the proximal interphalangeal joints of the index, middle, ring and little fingers and then apply back- and-forth rolling manipulation."
* #2250 #3183 #3188 "一指禪推法" "A tuina manipulation to exert constant force on the area to be treated with the tip or the palmar side of the thumb through active swing of the forearm."
* #2250 #3183 #3189 "一指禪偏鋒推法" "A variation of Yi Zhi Chan pushing manipulation using the radial side of the thumb."
* #2250 #3183 #3190 "纏法" "A fast Yi Zhi Chan pushing manipulation with a frequency of more than 220 times/min."
* #2250 #3183 #3191 "跪推法" "A variation of Yi Zhi Chan pushing manipulation with the radial or dorsal side of the interphalangeal joint of the thumb."
* #2250 #3183 #3192 "魚際揉法" "A tuina manipulation to knead and exert force on the area to be treated with the great thenar eminence through rhythmic swing of the forearm."
* #2250 #3183 #3193 "揉法" "A tuina manipulation to apply perpendicular pressure to the surface of the body with the fingers, palms or elbows. The pressure is supposed to reach subcutaneous tissues. It includes kneading with the finger, kneading with the palm and kneading with the forearm."
* #2250 #3183 #3194 "摩法" "A Tuina manipulation to rub the area to be treated circularly with the fingers or palms."
* #2250 #3183 #3195 "推法" "A one-way linear pushing manipulation with the fingers, palms, or elbows."
* #2250 #3183 #3196 "刨推法" "A pushing manipulation with the Hukou area (formed between the thumb and index finger)."
* #2250 #3183 #3197 "刮法" "A tuina manipulation to scrape with the radial side of the index finger, on the back of the interphalangeal joint of the index finger. Alternatively, scraping can be done with tools such as spoons or coins."
* #2250 #3183 #3198 "擦法" "A tuina manipulation to apply linear rubbing with the hypothenar, great thenar, palms or fingers."
* #2250 #3183 #3199 "抹法" "A tuina manipulation to apply top-down, left-right or arch-shaped wiping with the palmar side of the thumbs or the palms."
* #2250 #3183 #3200 "按法" "A tuina manipulation to apply perpendicular pressure to the surface of the body with the palmar side of the fingers, palms or elbows. It is also one of the common diagnostic methods in tuina practice."
* #2250 #3183 #3201 "點法" "A tuina manipulation to apply perpendicular pressure to the surface of the body with finger tips, interphalangeal joints or elbows."
* #2250 #3183 #3202 "壓法" "A tuina manipulation to apply continuous, forceful pressure to the surface of the body with the palmar side of the thumbs, palms or elbows."
* #2250 #3183 #3203 "抵法" "A tuina manipulation to apply symmetrical pressure to the area to be treated with the fingers or palms."
* #2250 #3183 #3204 "碟轉法" "A tuina manipulation to apply rotating pressure to the area to be treated with the palms."
* #2250 #3183 #3205 "拳頂法" "A tuina manipulation to apply pressure to the area to be treated with a fist."
* #2250 #3183 #3206 "捫法" "A tuina manipulation to rub the palms and gently place (the warm palms) over the area to be treated."
* #2250 #3183 #3207 "押法" "A tuina manipulation to press the area to be treated with the palmar side of the thumbs."
* #2250 #3183 #3208 "捏法" "A tuina manipulation to apply coordinated pressure to the area to be treated with the thumb and the other four fingers."
* #2250 #3183 #3209 "挪法" "A tuina manipulation to place the palm over the area to be treated, grasp and lift the muscle either using the thumb and the other four fingers or exerting a symmetric force from the base of the palm and fingers, hold for a while and then shift forward to repeat the procedure."
* #2250 #3183 #3210 "擰法" "A tuina manipulation to clamp and release the area to be treated with flexed index finger and middle finger or with the thumb and flexed index finger."
* #2250 #3183 #3211 "勾法" "A tuina manipulation to place the index finger and middle finger together and flex into a hook, stick to the skin with the radial border of the middle phalanx of index finger and then apply continuous pushing and wiping manipulation."
* #2250 #3183 #3212 "拿法" "A tuina manipulation to pinch and knead the area to be treated with the thumb and the other four fingers."
* #2250 #3183 #3213 "抓法" "A tuina manipulation to grab and pinch the area to be treated with five fingertips."
* #2250 #3183 #3214 "握法" "A tuina manipulation to clutch and release the limb with the palm."
* #2250 #3183 #3215 "搓法" "A tuina manipulation to hold the limb with both hands/palms and apply alternating or back-and- forth twisting."
* #2250 #3183 #3216 "理法" "A tuina manipulation to apply rhythmic grasping and pinching of the limb."
* #2250 #3183 #3217 "撚法" "A tuina manipulation to hold the area to be treated with the thumb and index finger and apply back-and-forth twisting."
* #2250 #3183 #3218 "撥法" "A tuina manipulation to press the area to be treated with fingers or elbows and then apply perpendicular plucking force to the subcutaneous tissue."
* #2250 #3183 #3219 "拍法" "A tuina manipulation to pat the area to be treated with the palms or fingers."
* #2250 #3183 #3220 "擊法" "A tuina manipulation to knock the surface of the body with the fist, palms, fingers or mulberry sticks."
* #2250 #3183 #3221 "啄法" "A tuina manipulation to place the five fingers together and peck the area to be treated."
* #2250 #3183 #3222 "叩法" "A tuina manipulation to tap the area to be treated with the ulnar side of the little finger or a hollow fist."
* #2250 #3183 #3223 "劈法" "A tuina manipulation to chop the area between the fingers with the ulnar side of the palm."
* #2250 #3183 #3224 "貫法" "A tuina manipulation to place one palm over the area to be treated and percuss the dorsum of the hand with a hollow fist made by the other hand."
* #2250 #3183 #3225 "彈法" "A tuina manipulation to flip the area to be treated with fingernails or palmar sides of the fingers."
* #2250 #3183 #3226 "抖法" "A tuina manipulation to grasp the affected limb and apply small-amplitude radial shakings."
* #2250 #3183 #3227 "振法" "A tuina manipulation to press acupuncture points or certain body parts with the fingers or palms and apply fast, continuous vibration."
* #2250 #3183 #3228 "搖法" "A tuina manipulation to conduct passive rotation of the affected joint along the axis of motion."
* #2250 #3183 #3229 "拔伸法；牽引法" "A tuina manipulation to immobilize one end of the affected joint or limb and pull the other end along the longitudinal axis."
* #2250 #3183 #3230 "勒法" "A tuina manipulation to clamp the affected fingers or toes with the fingers and apply rapid pulling."
* #2250 #3183 #3231 "屈伸法" "A tuina manipulation to slowly and repeatedly flex and extend the joints to stretch the surrounding tissues, and increase the joint range of motion."
* #2250 #3183 #3232 "扳法" "A tuina manipulation to apply a sudden thrust force to the affected joint to produce passive rotation, flexion, extension, abduction and adduction."
* #2250 #3183 #3233 "背法" "A tuina manipulation to carry the patient on the back and apply traction, shaking, vibration and instant extension to the affected lumbar vertebrae."
* #2250 #3183 #3234 "梳法" "A tuina manipulation to place the palmar sides of the fingers over the area to be treated and apply soft, one-way combing."
* #2250 #3183 #3235 "拂法" "A tuina manipulation to brush or scrub the surface of the body with the fingers."
* #2250 #3183 #3236 "捋法" "A tuina manipulation to place the palm or fingers over the area to be treated, gradually push from the proximal end to the distal end, and then release the force quickly."
* #2250 #3183 #3237 "掩法" "A tuina manipulation to gently cover the area to be treated with the hand and remain still."
* #2250 #3183 #3238 "捩法" "A tuina manipulation to make a passive rotation of the joints."
* #2250 #3183 #3239 "端法" "A tuina manipulation to hold or lift the affected limb with one or both hands."
* #2250 #3183 #3240 "摸法" "A tuina manipulation to press the limbs or acupuncture points with fingers to help with diagnosis."
* #2250 #3183 #3241 "提法" "A tuina manipulation to lift the post-traumatic sunken bones or joints to normal position using one or both hands or with the help of a rope."
* #2250 #3183 #3242 "攏法" "A tuina manipulation to clamp and release the skin of the area to be treated with the ulnar aspects of both palms."
* #2250 #3183 #3243 "擠法；挾按法" "A tuina manipulation to squeeze both sides of the area to be treated with one or both hands."
* #2250 #3183 #3244 "挽法" "A tuina manipulation to pull or move the limbs of the patient."
* #2250 #3183 #3245 "推拿複合手法類"
* #2250 #3183 #3246 "按揉法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of pressing and kneading manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3247 "拿揉法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of grasping and kneading manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3248 "推摩法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of Yi Zhi Chan pushing using the radial side of the thumb and rubbing circularly with the other four fingers."
* #2250 #3183 #3249 "牽抖法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of pulling-stretching and shaking manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3250 "揉捏法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of kneading and pinching manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3251 "提彈法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of tendon-flicking and muscle plucking manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3252 "彈筋法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of pinching and lifting manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3253 "推揉法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of Yi Zhi Chan pushing and kneading manipulation."
* #2250 #3183 #3254 "推扳法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of plucking and pulling manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3255 "勾點法" "A compound tuina manipulation composed of hooking and point-pressing manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3256 "掃散法" "A tuina manipulation to push and scrub along the Shaoyang meridian in the temporal region using the radial side of the thumb and tips of the other four fingers."
* #2250 #3183 #3257 "踩蹺法" "A tuina manipulation to step with the feet on certain areas of the body."
* #2250 #3183 #3258 "捏脊法" "A tuina manipulation to pinch and lift the skin of the spine symmetrically with the thumb, index finger and middle finger or with the thumb and the other four fingers and release the force immediately. Move forward gradually and repeat the same procedure."
* #2250 #3183 #3259 "點脊法" "A tuina manipulation to press the bladder meridian on both sides of the spine."
* #2250 #3183 #3260 "插法" "A tuina manipulation to insert the fingers into the space between the scapula and thoracic wall. It is specifically used for gastroptosis."
* #2250 #3183 #3261 "托法" "A tuina manipulation to lift the affected area with one or both hands. It is commonly used for gastroptosis."
* #2250 #3183 #3262 "抄法" "A tuina manipulation: Place the palms on the lower borders of the subcostal region and extend gradually to the low back, press and knead the soft tissues on both sides of the low back using the palmar sides of the fingers, lift the low back and abdomen and then swing to the left and right gently."
* #2250 #3183 #3263 "封腰法" "A tuina manipulation to place the thumbs and the middle fingers over the lumbar triangles on both sides and apply soft, gentle pressure."
* #2250 #3183 #3264 "顫腰法" "A tuina manipulation to press the low back coupled with traction and stretching."
* #2250 #3183 #3265 "脊柱推拿手法類" "Manipulations used on the spine to correct spinal subluxation and regulate the functions of the spine."
* #2250 #3183 #3266 "推拿介質" "Lotions applied to the surface of the body for lubrication or treatment."
* #2250 #3183 #3267 "推拿輔助器具類" "Supplementary tools to assist in tuina treatment and health care."
* #2250 #3183 #3268 "桑枝棒" "An assistant tuina tool made of 12 specific mulberry branches based on certain requirements with a strong effect to unblock and circulate qi and blood. It is often used on the top of the head, chest and back, waist, hip and limbs."
* #2250 #3183 #3269 "太平車" "An assistant tuina tool comprising an iron bar with five to six pieces of jade or sandalwood made into a chain of beads. The head and tail of the iron bar are folded. The appliance is connected to a short handle and can be held in the hand and the beads work like a rolling wheel. It is often used on the face, bone joints, etc."
* #2250 #3183 #3270 "小兒推拿" "A type of tuina therapy based on the theory of traditional Chinese medicine, using specific manipulations on the meridians and collaterals and specific points on the surface of the body. It helps to prevent against diseases and benefit health through harmonizing Zang–Fu organs, yin, yang, qi and blood, strengthen the body and promote the healthy growth and development of children."
* #2250 #3183 #3271 "少林內功" "A main training method in tuina exercise and a key part of Neigong tuina. It is originally a form of martial art to strengthen the body, but now becomes a distinctive method and school of self- exercise plus tuina treatment."
* #2250 #3183 #3272 "易筋經" "A traditional health-building exercise to stretch the body, strengthen the sinews, bones and ligaments and regulate the meridians and zang–fu organs."
* #2250 #3183 #3273 "其他推拿術語"
* #2250 #3183 #3274 "熱敷" "A common external therapy in Chinese medicine to treat diseases through heat stimulation."
* #2250 #3183 #3275 "膏摩" "A therapeutic method to apply herbal ointment on the surface of the area to be treated, followed by tuina manipulations."
* #2250 #3183 #3276 "推拿鎮痛" "One of the pain control methods in Chinese medicine by applying Tuina to reduce or control pain according to theories of Chinese medicine."
* #2250 #3183 #3277 "健身推拿" "A tuina method for healthy people or people with suboptimal health to strengthen the body and help with disease prevention and treatment."
* #2250 #3183 #3278 "美容推拿" "A type of tuina method to achieve healthier skin and slow down the ageing process of the skin."
* #2250 #3279 "3.7 導引氣功"
* #2250 #3279 #3280 "導引" "To guide qi to harmonize the body and stretch the body to make it soft. It aims to prevent and cure diseases and promote health through stretching exercise combined with breath and internal qi circulation."
* #2250 #3279 #3281 "氣功" "An exercise method to regulate the body, breath and mind to achieve body-mind, man-society and man-nature harmonization. It cultivates the essence, qi and spirit internally and sinews, bones and body externally according to ancient Chinese philosophy."
* #2250 #3279 #3282 "大周天" "Originated in Daoism, it refers to the second stage of internal alchemical process (i.e., to refine qi to spirit). This is performed on the basis of the small heavenly circle exercise and is believed that the large heavenly circle can connect spirit with qi, improve health and attain longevity."
* #2250 #3279 #3283 "三關" "he three areas in the ascending path along the u meridian when internal qi circulates along he pathways of the Du and Ren meridians uring practicing the“small heavenly circle”(or microcosmic orbit”) meditation. They are rduous for internal qi to overcome and therefore alled“passes”or“barriers”.Alternatively, hey refer to the three steps in internal alchemy Neidan): refining the essence to qi; refining the i to spirit; and refining the spirit to emptiness. ome also use three passes to represent the ears, yes and mouth."
* #2250 #3279 #3284 "和合四象" "A specific state in qigong practice that requires dropping the eyelids, listening to the silence, regulating breathing by focusing the eyes on the tip of the nose and closing the mouth. This state helps to remove distracting thoughts during qigong practice."
* #2250 #3279 #3285 "恬淡虛無" "A state of peaceful inner joy and free from ego or desires. This principle on health cultivation was recorded in the Huang Di Nei Jing (Yellow Emperor’ s Internal Classic) and highly valued by generations of qigong masters."
* #2250 #3279 #3286 "丹田呼吸" "A beneficial variation of natural breathing with the Dantian area. Unlike usual abdominal breathing, this breathing method does not need mental focus or external force."
* #2250 #3279 #3287 "3.8 太極" "Taiji is an ancient Chinese philosophical term which represents the state of undifferentiated absolute and infinite potential; the oneness before duality, from which yin and yang, the basic polarity of the universe, originate. It has later become the highest conceivable principle about the generation, development and changes of all things in the universe. Taiji is often considered as the root and symbol of traditional Chinese culture. It has far-reaching impact on the Yi Jing (the Book of Changes), traditional Chinese medicine and taijiquan."
* #2250 #3279 #3288 "太極拳" "An ancient Chinese martial art practiced for both defence training and health benefits. It is a combination of breathing, mental focus, and physical movements, whose intention is to cultivate the practitioner’ s internal qi. It is rooted in the philosophies of Confucianism, Daoism and Chinese medicine, and concepts of Taiji, yin-yang and the Five Elements."
* #2250 #3279 #3289 "太極拳流派" "Major influential taijiquan styles include Chen style, yang style, Wú style, Wǔ style, Sun style and Zhaobao style."
* #2250 #3279 #3290 "太極拳拳理" "The principles of taijiquan are based on its philosophical and medical meaning as well as practice requirements. They include Taiji theories, mnemonics and classics."
* #2250 #3279 #3291 "拳決" "The tips or smart ways in Taiji practice. These include mnemonics for taijiquan, Taiji sword, Taiji spear, Taiji knife and Taiji pushing hands. These mnemonics are often vivid and conversational."
* #2250 #3279 #3292 "拳譜" "A catalogue to record categories or systems. There are three types of Taiji manuals: 1) the names of sequenced Taiji skills and weapons routines/ forms; 2) Inheritance lineage; and 3) books on Taiji skills."
* #2250 #3279 #3293 "拳經" "Classics/texts which are exclusively focussed on the essential principles that guide the practice of taijiquan."
* #2250 #3279 #3294 "拳勢" "Intangible frame and rhythm, including physical moves, eye movement and connections or transitions between moves."
* #2250 #3279 #3295 "內家拳" "It refers to Wudang martial arts (occupied with spiritual, mental or qi-related aspects), as opposed to Shaolin (external) martial arts (focused on physical aspects). There are three internal martial arts: taijiquan, xingyiquan (form- intention fist) and baguazhang (eight trigram palm)."
* #2250 #3279 #3296 "盤架子" "A series of movements in Taiji practice."
* #2250 #3279 #3297 "八面" "hese include four sides and four corners. If we onsider taijiquan as a 3-D ball, symmetrical elease of forces from eight directions (left, right, p, down and two diagonal angles) is essential o maintain the body equilibrium, known as the rounded force”."
* #2250 #3279 #3298 "八體" "Vertex, crotch, heart, eyes, ears, hands, feet and waist."
* #2250 #3279 #3299 "八不傳" "Those with no loyalty or filial piety; those with no humanistic spirit; those with evil intentions; those who are reckless and impulsive; those who are defiant and arrogant; those who are rude and disrespectful; those who are unpredictable; and those who care too much about gains and losses."
* #2250 #3279 #3300 "四忌" "Avoid using strength to store qi; avoid expanding the chest or sinking the low back; avoid shrugging the shoulders or sinking the neck; avoid discontinuity in body moves."
* #2250 #3279 #3301 "八要" "Relax the shoulders and sink the elbows; lift the vertex and issue force from the thighs; tap the teeth and touch the palate with the tongue; sink the chest and loosen the waist; move naturally; internal-external unity; yin-yang coordination; and stillness in movement."
* #2250 #3279 #3302 "無極" "An ancient philosophical term, referring to the primordial universe prior to the Taiji (supreme ultimate). In Taiji practice, it has three extended meanings: 1) a relatively static state; 2) mental serenity before body moves in Taiji practice; 3) abdomen."
* #2250 #3279 #3303 "兩儀" "An ancient philosophical term, referring to yin and yang. In Taiji practice, the two forms have three extended meanings: 1) movement and stillness; 2) symmetrical Taiji moves; 3) the two kidneys."
* #2250 #3279 #3304 "三才" "An ancient philosophical term, referring to heaven, earth and man. In Taiji practice, it is used to represent head, hands and feet, further indicating the upper, middle and lower body parts."
* #2250 #3279 #3305 "四象" "An ancient philosophical term. It has two meanings in Taiji: 1) upper and lower limbs; 2) four directions—front, back, left and right."
* #2250 #3279 #3306 "體用" "These include health benefits and self-defence training. Health benefits are achieved through practicing taolu (solo hand and weapons routines/ forms). Self-defence training mainly refers to pushing hands sequences, which embody a variety of self-defence techniques."
* #2250 #3279 #3307 "六合" "The six closings include three internal harmonies (intent, qi and strength) and three external ones (shoulder-hip, elbow-knee and hands-feet). These harmonies allow the movements to connect seamlessly and the force to be intact."
* #2250 #3279 #3308 "太極圖" "A symbol or diagram in Chinese philosophy representing Taiji and its dualist aspects—yin and yang."
* #2250 #3279 #3309 "虛領頂勁；提頂；頂頭懸；貫頂；懸頂" "The head is held as if suspended by a thread attached to the crown, at the point Baihui (Du20). This posture aims to avoid tension in the neck. In doing so, do not tilt the head from side to side, or up and down. The whole body is light and nimble when the head is suspended at the crown."
* #2250 #3279 #3310 "沉肩墜肘" "Relax and drop down the shoulders, especially the acromion, or the point Jianjing (GB 21); at the same time, slightly flex and drop the elbow."
* #2250 #3279 #3311 "護肫" "A technique to protect the stomach in Taiji practice. It produces a similar effect as dropping the elbow."
* #2250 #3279 #3312 "展指坐腕" "xtend and relax the fingers naturally, slightly eparating them to form an arc or spiral at the ack of the hand. This enables the internal i to radiate to the fingers and is known as materializing in the hands and fingers”."
* #2250 #3279 #3313 "兩膊相系" "During Taiji practice, the movement of one arm triggers corresponding movement in the other one, as if they are connected to each other by a cord."
* #2250 #3279 #3314 "豎腕" "Keep the wrist and fingers in a vertical position (approximately 90º). However, it is advisable not to use force to bend the wrist."
* #2250 #3279 #3315 "坐腕" "Drop and relax the wrist to allow the internal energy to reach the fingertips from the arm."
* #2250 #3279 #3316 "鼓腕" "Pump the wrist to soften and relax the hands."
* #2250 #3279 #3317 "直腕" "Make the wrist straight and flat."
* #2250 #3279 #3318 "疊腕" "Place one wrist over the other."
* #2250 #3279 #3319 "含胸拔背" "Intentionally relax the chest and back muscles and intercostal space. The shoulders should be naturally rounded to allow the chest to be slightly hollowed. To“raise the back”means to stretch the spine longitudinally and horizontally to allow the qi to stick to the back (so that one can use this qi to issue great power)."
* #2250 #3279 #3320 "擴背" "Expanding the back is closely associated with sinking the chest. By sinking the chest, one will be able to expand the back naturally."
* #2250 #3279 #3321 "松腰斂臀" "To relax the waist means to lift the coccyx and sink the points Shensu (BL 23) and Mingmen (DU 4). To tuck buttocks under means to slightly tuck in the buttocks."
* #2250 #3279 #3322 "弓腰收臀" "By sinking the waist in a relaxed sitting position, one will be able to tuck in the buttocks naturally."
* #2250 #3279 #3323 "尾閭中正" "During Taiji practice, the coccyx needs to be kept upright at all times."
* #2250 #3279 #3324 "尾閭收；提肛；吊襠；調襠" "To lift the anus, tuck in the coccyx and keep the body neutral. This helps to maintain balance and feel comfortable."
* #2250 #3279 #3325 "上下一線" "To make Baihui (DU 20) and Huiyin (REN 1) vertically connected, in order to keep the upper body aligned and upright; and to connect Jianjing (GB 21) with Yongquan (KI 1) to keep the head and feet vertically connected."
* #2250 #3279 #3326 "立身中正" "To keep the torso perpendicular to the ground and place the bodyweight over the supporting feet. This helps to keep the body stable."
* #2250 #3279 #3327 "身肢放長" "To lengthen the torso through“holding the head as if suspended from above”.To extend the arms through sinking the shoulders and dropping the elbows. To lengthen the legs through relaxing the waist, opening the hips and flexing the knees."
* #2250 #3279 #3328 "一身備五弓" "The five bows include the torso, both hands and both feet. The body bow uses the waist as the handle and uses Yamen (DU 15) and coccyx as the tips. The hand bow uses the elbow as the handle and uses the wrist and clavicle as the tips. The foot bow uses the knee as the handle and uses the hip and heel as the tips."
* #2250 #3279 #3329 "泛臀" "To attempt to float the buttocks before issuing internal energy."
* #2250 #3279 #3330 "溜臀" "To relax and drop the buttocks when squatting down."
* #2250 #3279 #3331 "斂臀" "To slightly tuck in the buttocks to facilitate the abdomen and relax the crotch. This helps to keep the body neutral and stable."
* #2250 #3279 #3332 "圓襠" "To relax the hip joint, relax hip muscles and tuck in the femoral head. This enables the crotch to become round."
* #2250 #3279 #3333 "縮胯" "To relax and, at the same time, tuck in the hips to enable the perineum to become a semicircle."
* #2250 #3279 #3334 "裹襠" "To adduct the knees intentionally to protect the crotch. This helps to distinguish empty from solid, keep the lower body stable and increase the loading ability of the legs. This can also lengthen the legs."
* #2250 #3279 #3335 "蕩襠" "A common error in Taiji practice. Slack crotch may cause energy to scatter."
* #2250 #3279 #3336 "尖襠" "A common error in Taiji practice. A sharp-angled crotch may cause an inability to support the internal energy."
* #2250 #3279 #3337 "敞襠" "A common error in Taiji practice. An open crotch (>90º) may cause an inability to obtain the internal energy."
* #2250 #3279 #3338 "坐腿" "To appropriately flex the knee to enable the body weight to be placed on the flexed leg."
* #2250 #3279 #3339 "開合" "Movement combination of the body, breathing and energy. The“opening”refers to eccentric movements of the arms, whereas the“closing” refers to concentric movements of the arms. Breathing in and releasing energy act to open, whereas breathing out and storing energy act to close."
* #2250 #3279 #3340 "蓄髮" "To“store energy”means to relax muscles with mental focus. To“release energy”means to contract muscles and often combine with breathing."
* #2250 #3279 #3341 "動靜" "A dynamic state in Taiji practice. Taiji movements are open, continuous and transitional like a river; however, they can also close and become static like mountains."
* #2250 #3279 #3342 "快慢" "Taiji movements are relatively slower than other martial arts; the speed of movement varies among differing styles."
* #2250 #3279 #3343 "虛實分清；虛實分明" "In Taiji practice, it is important to distinguish between fullness and emptiness all over the body (i.e. we must know exactly which part of our body is issuing power and which part is receiving it). As for the legs, the“empty”leg is never absolutely weightless, and the“full”leg does not have all the weight placed upon it."
* #2250 #3279 #3344 "開合虛實" "The principles of Taiji exercise. The“opening” means to stretch, step forward, bend down and drop. The“closing”means to flex, step backward, raise and lift. The“empty”means absence of mental focus, supplementary force, relaxation and closing movements. The“solid”means presence of mental focus, major force point and opening movements."
* #2250 #3279 #3345 "內外相合" "The coordination or harmony between mental intent and physical movement. The“unity”here means a perfect and natural combination between hands, feet and mental intent."
* #2250 #3279 #3346 "上下相隨" "Coordinated movements of the upper and lower limbs as well as the torso. Hand movements, waist movements, leg movements, and even the eye movements — all are in one unified movement (The root is in the feet, released through the legs, controlled by the waist, and materialized in the hands and fingers)."
* #2250 #3279 #3347 "節節貫串" "This refers to successive movement of nine major joints throughout the body: neck, back, low back, hip, knee, ankle, shoulder, elbow and wrist."
* #2250 #3279 #3348 "輕靈" "A distinctive feature of taijiquan. Mindfulness or“intent”guides the body movement and breathing; both breathing and internal qi are controlled by the mindfulness of the will. The “gentle”means to guide movements with intent instead of force. The“flexible”means to move the arms or legs with agility."
* #2250 #3279 #3349 "一動無有不動" "The power is rooted in the feet, controlled by the waist, and manifested through the fingers, integrating the body as one harmonious unit."
* #2250 #3279 #3350 "剛柔" "Force and changes of taijiquan. The“hard”refers to fast, short release of force in an attack, whereas the“soft”refers to slow, gentle and spiral moves in defence."
* #2250 #3279 #3351 "方圓" "The two technical features of Taiji. The“square” refers to the force points, whereas the“round” refers to arc-shaped Taiji movements. The two features are unity of opposites."
* #2250 #3279 #3352 "方圓相生" "The“square”refers to the hard power point that manifests in four sides and four corners. The “round”refers to the soft, circulating qi. The “square”originates from the“round”,and the “round”contains the“square”.There is“square” within the“round”and vice versa."
* #2250 #3279 #3353 "十三勢" "These include eight force-releasing methods and five footwork methods. The eight force-releasing methods include four straight directions (Peng, Lǚ, Ji and An) and four diagonal directions (Cai, Lie, Zhou and Kao). The five footwork methods are called step forward, step backward, gaze (that is, focus your intention toward and move) to the left, gaze to the right, and central equilibrium."
* #2250 #3279 #3354 "太極勁" "A collective term for a variety of power or energy developed through Taiji practice."
* #2250 #3279 #3355 "中土常守" "he“Zhong”means the centre in direction. The Tu”means earth in five elements. Together hey mean the“centre”or body weight. In Taiji ractice, it is important to distinguish empty and ull in all body parts as well as the two legs."
* #2250 #3279 #3356 "四兩撥千斤" "A force of four ounces deflects a thousand ounds’(i.e. to repel force effortlessly)."
* #2250 #3279 #3357 "勁與力" "The“Jin”originally means strong and powerful. In Taiji practice, it refers to the cultivated, trained and flexible force that can change in power, direction, force point and force speed, in response to an opponent’ s attack."
* #2250 #3279 #3358 "氣沉丹田" "To sink qi to Dantian, you need to keep the coccyx upright, sink the chest, relax the shoulders and suspend the crotch, and thus to guide qi down to the abdomen with intent."
* #2250 #3279 #3359 "開呼合吸" "An essential principle in coordinated movements and breathing: breathe out when performing opening, solid, stretching, stepping forward, dropping, bending down, moving away, releasing, attacking or striking moves; and breathe in when performing closing, empty, sinking, flexing, stepping backward, lifting, moving back, raising, collecting, and softening moves."
* #2250 #3279 #3360 "開吸合呼" "An essential principle in coordinated movements and breathing: breathe in when opening up the rib cage and breathe out when closing the rib cage."
* #2250 #3279 #3361 "意氣運動" "A distinctive feature of taijiquan. The“intent” means to guide the body movement and breathing with mental intent. The“qi”means both breathing and internal qi are controlled by mental intent."
* #2250 #3279 #3362 "神氣鼓蕩" "A distinctive feature of taijiquan. The“Shen” includes spirit and eye direction. The“qi” means breathing and internal qi move with body movement."
* #2250 #3279 #3363 "太極九如" "The nine essentials in Taiji practice. These include: 1) keep the head upright as if suspended by a thread; 2) keep the coccyx upright as if pillared to the ground; 3) raise the upper back as if carrying a pot on the back ; 4) sink the chest like holding a tree; 5) suspend the crotch as if holding a ball; 6) relax the waist; 7) move the hands as if tearing cotton; 8) move the feet as if sailing a boat; and 9) move the legs as if pulling a string."
* #2250 #3279 #3364 "鬆緊" "An important feature of Taiji practice. There is a saying that it takes at least 10 years to really understand Taiji. The key part is learning to cultivate internal energy through relaxation and stillness. It is necessary to avoid tension and mental stress before achieving relaxation in Taiji practice."
* #2250 #3279 #3365 "齊四梢" "Intentionally projecting internal force through the tips of fingers and toes."
* #2250 #3279 #3366 "功法" "Basic skills acquired through learning standard Taiji moves and pushing hands. These require continuous practice and include both psychological and physiological abilities."
* #2250 #3279 #3367 "太極劍" "A weaponry (sword) form of Taiji practice, based on ancient fencing technique, coupled with fencing arts from other schools of martial arts."
* #2250 #3279 #3368 "太極扇" "A combination of taijiquan and skills of playing with a fan. It moves or rotates the fan with intent and acts to benefit health, uplift the mood, and entertain yourself and others."
* #2250 #3279 #3369 "太極槍" "A weaponry (spear) form of Taiji practice. The spear is long, sharp and easy to use. It is known as the“king of weapons”because of the significant military advantage it provides on battlefields."
* #2250 #3279 #3370 "太極棍" "A Taiji practice, which uses a cane or stick as a weapon. The stick is known as the“head of weapons”.There were no standard Taiji cane forms before 1949, with the exception of the White Ape form in Chen style Taiji. After 1949, martial arts masters including Cui Yishi developed Taiji cane forms."
* #2250 #3279 #3371 "太極推手" "Also known as hitting hands, kneading hands or crossing hands, pushing hands is a two-person training routine practiced in Taiji. It works to undo a person’ s natural instinct to resist force with force, teaching the body to yield to force and redirect it. There are mainly eight methods in practicing pushing hands: bing-warding off; lǚ-rolling back; ji-pressing; an-pushing; cai- plucking; lie-splitting; zhou- elbow striking; and kao-shoulder striking."
* #2250 #3279 #3372 "掤勁" "Rising or expanding internal energy. With this energy, one feels the body is full of qi in all eight directions. During pushing hands, this energy allows you to create a buffer zone that prevents the first shock of an incoming attack and at the same time, enable you to defend, absorb or redirect the opponent’ s power."
* #2250 #3279 #3373 "捋勁" "The use of force in a sideways direction, such as where we intercept and move with a forward directed attack, simultaneously diverting it slightly to one side and thus to empty space."
* #2250 #3279 #3374 "擠勁" "Internal energy that projects forward from its source to prevent your opponent from issuing strength. During pushing hands, this energy radiates along the outside of the forearms and the back of the hands."
* #2250 #3279 #3375 "按勁" "A forward and downward movement of energy. During pushing hands, this energy enables you to prevent your opponent from raising their arms or legs, and at the same time, allows you to stick to and redirect the opponent’ s power."
* #2250 #3279 #3376 "采勁" "Internal energy that grasps or pulls the opponent’ s wrist or elbow downwards with the fingertips or palm."
* #2250 #3279 #3377 "挒勁" "Internal energy to push or pluck forward. During pushing hands, this energy allows you to use a spiral motion to split the opponent’s body and destroy the opponent’ s balance."
* #2250 #3279 #3378 "肘勁" "Internal energy that projects through the forearm rotation and tip of the elbow. During pushing hands, you can either defend the opponent’ s attack with the tip of your elbow. Alternatively, you can use one hand to stick to the opponent’ s hand and use flexed elbow of the other arm to spirally press the opponent’ s elbow and arm."
* #2250 #3279 #3379 "靠勁" "Internal energy to squeeze forward with your shoulder, back and hip. During pushing hands, this energy allows you to push the opponent with your shoulder, back or hip."
* #2250 #3279 #3380 "雙浮" "A wrong move in pushing hands."
* #2250 #3279 #3381 "雙重" "Rigid, inflexible moves during pushing hands."
* #2250 #3279 #3382 "雙輕" "Connection and transition between empty and solid moves."
* #2250 #3279 #3383 "單重" "This is opposed to a stance with equal weight on both feet. Single weighted stance refers to the body weight focusing on one foot."
* #2250 #3279 #3384 "掤法" "One of the eight methods in pushing hands: An upward circular movement, forward or backward, yielding or offsetting usually with the arms to disrupt the opponent’ s centre of gravity."
* #2250 #3279 #3385 "捋法" "One of the eight methods in pushing hands: to apply sideways, circular yielding movement to the opponent’ s wrist with one hand and stick to the opponent’ s elbow with the other hand."
* #2250 #3279 #3386 "擠法" "One of the eight methods in pushing hands: A pressing or squeezing offset in a direction away from the body, usually done with the back of the hand or outside edge of the forearm."
* #2250 #3279 #3387 "采法" "One of the eight methods in pushing hands: to grasp the opponent’ s wrist or elbow downwards with the fingertips or palm."
* #2250 #3279 #3388 "挒法" "One of the eight methods in pushing hands: to separate, to twist or to offset with a spiral motion, often while making immobile part of the body (such as a hand or leg) to split an opponent’ s body thereby destroying posture and balance."
* #2250 #3279 #3389 "肘法" "One of the eight methods in pushing hands: to strike or push with the elbow."
* #2250 #3279 #3390 "靠法" "One of the eight methods in pushing hands: to strike or push with the shoulder, upper back, arm or hip."
* #2250 #3279 #3391 "粘走" "wo basic skills in the practice of Taiji pushing ands. To“adhere”means to get your opponent o follow you under your control; whereas to follow”means to obey your opponent’ s ntention by letting him think he can apply an pplication, which leads him to use force."
* #2250 #3279 #3392 "太極刀" "A weaponry (broadsword) form of Taiji practice, which includes the philosophy, training and thirteen standard forms."
* #2250 #3393 "3.9 情志療法" "Methods to treat disordered moods and emotions using Chinese medicine theories on emotions."
* #2250 #3393 #3394 "祝由法" "A psychotherapeutic treatment protocol that combines analysis of causative factors of mental- emotional disorders with prayer. These analyses and prayer treatments are based on emotion theories and psychological principles in Chinese medicine."
* #2250 #3393 #3395 "情志相勝法" "A psychotherapeutic method that uses the mutual restraint cycle of five element theory in the treatment of mental-emotional disorders, during which the doctor intentionally arouses certain feelings in the patient that counteract their abnormal emotional state."
* #2250 #3393 #3396 "悲勝怒" "An emotion therapy based on the restraining cycle of the five-element theory: sadness corresponds to metal; anger corresponds to wood; metal restrains wood; so sad feelings and language will subdue or release anger."
* #2250 #3393 #3397 "怒勝思" "An emotion therapy based on the restraining cycle of the five-element theory: anger corresponds to wood; thinking corresponds to earth; and wood restrains earth; so provocation to elicit an angry response will relieve anxiety and over-thinking."
* #2250 #3393 #3398 "思勝恐" "An emotion therapy based on the restraining cycle of the five-element theory: thinking corresponds to earth; fear corresponds to water; earth restrains water; so encouraging mental focus or meditation can help the patient ignore or forget fear."
* #2250 #3393 #3399 "恐勝喜" "An emotion therapy based on the restraining cycle of the five-element theory: fear corresponds to water; joy corresponds to fire; water restrains fire; so eliciting a fearful response will restrain excess joy and euphoria."
* #2250 #3393 #3400 "喜勝悲" "An emotion therapy based on the restraining cycle of the five-element theory: joy corresponds to fire; sadness corresponds to metal; fire restrains metal; and so happiness can overcome melancholy and sorrow."
* #2250 #3393 #3401 "活套法" "Flexible psychotherapeutic methods that apply the mutual promotion or mutual restraint relationship among the five phases to different mental-emotional disorders. This idea was first mentioned in the Su Wen and later promulgated by Zhu Danxi."
* #2250 #3393 #3402 "驚者平之法" "A method to treat mental-emotional diseases such as anxiety, panic and post-traumatic stress disorders. Under controlled conditions, by repeated exposure to the circumstance that trigger the patient’ s anxiety or traumatic response disorder, their adverse reaction is gradually lessened and normalized."
* #2250 #3393 #3403 "五行音樂" "A method to use different qualities of the five musical tones (gong, shang, jiao, zheng and yu), related to the five elements in the treatment of mental disorders."
* #2250 #3393 #3404 "意療法" "A method to treat mental disorders by communicating with the patient’ s self-image."
* #2250 #3393 #3405 "五志" "Joy, anger, grief, excess thinking and fear."
* #2250 #3393 #3406 "五志過極" "Pathological conditions that result from excessive mental activity impairing the essential qi of five zang organs and/or disturbing qi movement within zang–fu organs."
* #2250 #3393 #3407 "驚則氣亂" "Sudden shock and fright disrupt the qi activity and causes qi movements to become confused and disorderly."
* #2250 #3393 #3408 "恐則氣下" "Excess fear or an ongoing state of fearfulness causes qi to strongly descend."
* #2250 #3393 #3409 "喜則氣緩" "Excess joy and/or euphoria cause qi to move too slowly and the qi movement becomes sluggish."
* #2250 #3393 #3410 "悲則氣消" "Excess sorrow and grief weaken the qi."
* #2250 #3393 #3411 "怒則氣上" "Rage and persistent anger cause disorderly ascending of the qi."
* #2250 #3412 "3.10 其他"
* #2250 #3412 #3413 "食療學" "The study of essential knowledge and use of diet as part of therapy."
* #2250 #3412 #3414 "藥膳" "Food prepared in combination with Chinese herbs to benefit the health, where the selection of the herbs is based on the theories of Chinese medicine."
* #2250 #3412 #3415 "食療；食治；食養；食補" "A therapeutic approach to harmonize qi and blood, balance yin and yang, prevent disease and benefit health, through the use of specifically tailored diets, where the properties of the food ingredients are guided by the theories of Chinese medicine."
